 sequoia mahmod samat ninerafaxstat nonobstructive berk gillmore vutrisiran transthyretin nejmoa2409134 lindenfeld rodes cabau interatrial girerd barbet seronde telesat ejhf malgie clephas titrate ayodele spahillari inclisiran leosdottir lindahl swedeheart klug llerena lerodalcibep bergmark marston prohaska olezarsen hypertriglyceridemia ballantyne vasas azizad plozasiran apoc3 muvalaplin e2424017 zerlasiran e2421957 lepodisiran acclaim nct06292013 nct06292013 olpasiran nct05581303 nct05581303 pelacarsen tqj230 nct04023552 nct04023552 wolski nonprescription upton zilebesiran kardia kolber bedmed e059711 salam schutte telmisartan indapamide taddei quadro perindopril indapamide bisoprolol kandzari','Abstract Introduction In 2024, numerous key clinical trials in the field of clinical cardiology have been published or presented at major international conferences. This review seeks to collate and summarise these trials and reflect on their clinical context. Methods The authors evaluated all clinical trials presented at major cardiology conferences during 2024 with a focus on clinical trials which would influence and/or change current clinical practice. We reviewed clinical trials presented at all major international conferences including the American College of Cardiology (ACC), European Association for Percutaneous Cardiovascular Interventions (EuroPCR), European Society of Cardiology (ESC), Transcatheter Cardiovascular Therapeutics (TCT), American Heart Association (AHA), European Heart Rhythm Association (EHRA), Society for Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions (SCAI), TVT-The Heart Summit (TVT) and Cardiovascular Research Technologies (CRT). Trials considered to have highest impact and/or broad relevance across the field of clinical cardiology, with a high likelihood to change or impact upon clinical practice were included. Results Over 90 key cardiology clinical trials were identified across the spectrum of clinical cardiology. Important updates in percutaneous coronary intervention were reviewed including new ESC guidance and several key trials in the field of coronary physiology (FAVOR III), drug-coated balloons (REGCAGE-FREE, AGENT-IDE), shock, and acute coronary syndromes (SENIOR-RITA, DanGer-Shock). Structural trials included major updates in transcatheter aortic valve replacement (TAVR) from EARLY-TAVR, TAVR-UNLOAD and NOTION 3, as well as seminal trials in tricuspid (TRISCEND II) and mitral intervention (MATTERHORN). Key updates in preventative cardiology included new data in lipoprotein ( a ) pharmacotherapy, low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol reduction and hypertension management (BPROAD, BedMed, KRAKEN), as well as several key trials in heart failure (SUMMIT, FINEARTS) hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (SEQUOIA-HCM) and cardiac amyloid (HELIOS-B). Conclusion The review presents a concise summary of the key clinical cardiology trials published or presented during the past year and should be of interest to clinicians and researchers in the field of cardiology. Keywords: Clinical trials, Cardiology, Acute coronary syndromes, Heart failure, Prevention, Electrophysiology, Structural, Intervention Key Summary Points This review paper presents a concise summary of over 90 key cardiology trials presented at major international conferences during 2024 with the potential to impact and change current practice. Important updates from across the full breadth of clinical cardiology were included, encompassing heart failure, prevention, coronary intervention, electrophysiology and structural intervention. Major updates were noted in management of stable coronary syndromes, intravascular imaging and antiplatelet strategies in addition to important advances in mitral and tricuspid intervention. Additionally, several key trials in heart failure and prevention were noted, covering novel therapies for cardiac amyloid, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, lipoprotein ( a ) modification and management of dyslipidaemia. Open in a new tab Introduction During 2024, several key trials with the potential to change clinical practice in cardiology were presented at major international meetings including the American College of Cardiology (ACC), European Association for Percutaneous Cardiovascular Interventions (EuroPCR), European Society of Cardiology (ESC), Transcatheter Cardiovascular Therapeutics (TCT), American Heart Association (AHA), European Heart Rhythm Association (EHRA), Society for Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions (SCAI), TVT-The Heart Summit (TVT) and Cardiovascular Research Technologies (CRT). This review seeks to provide a concise summary of the key studies presented and/or published in the field of clinical Cardiology during 2024, encompassing all major sub-specialities including acute coronary syndromes (ACS), percutaneous and structural intervention, electrophysiology, heart failure (HF) and prevention. Methods The results of clinical trials presented at major international cardiology meetings in 2024 were reviewed. Additionally, a literature search of PubMed, Medline, Cochrane Library and Embase was completed including the terms “acute coronary syndrome”, “atrial fibrillation”, “coronary prevention”, “electrophysiology”, “heart failure” and “interventional cardiology”. Trials were selected on the basis of their likelihood to impact and/or change current clinical guidelines and practice. Additionally, novel data with the scope to inform and guide further exploratory phase III studies were also included. This article is based on previously completed work and does not involve any new studies of human or animal subjects performed by any of the authors. Results Coronary Syndromes, Shock and Antiplatelet Strategies New guidance on chronic coronary syndromes (CCS) was published this year with several important updates [ 1 ]. Left main stem (LMS) revascularisation guidance was previously controversial because of lack of agreement between interventionists and the European Association of Cardio-thoracic surgeons (EACTS) regarding interpretation of the 5-year EXCEL data [ 2 ]. In the latest CCS guidance, consensus has been reached (based largely on long-term outcomes from a large individual-patient data [ n = 4393] meta-analysis [ 3 ]) that while both coronary-artery bypass grafting (CABG) and percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) are clinically reasonable, on the basis of patient preference, expertise availability, and local operator volumes (CABG class IA; PCI class IIa), CABG is preferred overall (class IA recommendation) vs. PCI owing to lower risk of spontaneous myocardial infarction (MI) and repeat revascularisation. However, PCI is recommended (class IA) for LMS revascularisation in patients with low SYNTAX (≤ 22) if equivalent completeness of revascularisation to CABG can be achieved owing to its lower invasiveness and non-inferior survival and may still be considered (class IIa) for patients with intermediate SYNTAX (23–32). Triple vessel disease (TVD) and left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) > 35% retains a class I indication to improve long-term survival vs. optimal medical therapy (OMT), as does CABG for patients with LVEF < 35%, with the caveat that for those at high surgical risk, PCI is recommended (class IIb, a downgrade from IIa). Intravascular imaging for the treatment of anatomically complex lesions (including LMS, true bifurcations and long lesions) now has a class IA recommendation . Additionally, short duration dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) (1–3 months) followed by clopidogrel monotherapy in patients with low ischaemic risk but high bleeding risk is now recommended for CCS (class Ia) [ 1 ]. Concern exists regarding the utility of PCI in an older population, with a paucity of data in this cohort of patients. The Older Patients With Non-ST-Segment Elevation Myocardial Infarction Randomized Interventional Treatment (SENIOR-RITA) study randomised 1518 patients with ACS ≥ 75 years (mean age 82 years; 45% women, 32% were frail, > 60% had cognitive impairment) to invasive care vs. medical therapy (with DAPT and standard post-MI therapy). At a median follow-up of 4.1 years, there was no difference in the primary composite endpoint of cardiovascular death (CVD) and non-fatal MI (25.6% vs. 26.3%: HR 0.94; 95% CI 0.77–1.14), but there was a significant 25% reduction in non-fatal MI (11.7% vs. 15.0%; HR 0.75; 95% CI 0.57–0.99) [ 4 ]. Notably, the trial was underpowered for the primary endpoint as fewer patients were recruited than planned as a result of COVID-19. Furthermore, while 90.3% of the invasive group underwent angiography only 46.6% of the invasive group (vs. 13.7% of the conservative group) actually received PCI, and only 30% of those with multivessel disease received complete revascularisation. SENIOR-RITA was thus a trial of strategy, rather than PCI. Interestingly, a meta-analysis comparing Complete Versus Culprit-Only Revascularization in Older Patients With ST-Segment Elevation Myocardial Infarction (EARTH STEMI), including seven randomised controlled trials (RCTs) in patients with STEMI ≥ 75 years (median age 79; 34% women), reported complete revascularisation was associated with a significant reduction in the primary composite endpoint of death, MI and revascularisation at 4 years (0.78; 95% CI 0.63–0.96; p = 0.005). Furthermore a marked reduction in the secondary composite endpoint of CVD or MI was noted (11.5% vs. 15.1%; adjusted HR 0.76; 95% CI 0.58–0.99) [ 5 ] thereby highlighting the importance of complete revascularisation in such patients. The role of mechanical circulatory support (MCS) in cardiogenic shock (CS) has been uncertain with disappointing results from previously discussed trials such as IABP-SHOCK II and ECLS-SHOCK [ 6 ]. The Microaxial Flow Pump in Infarct-Related Cardiogenic Shock (DanGer Shock) study randomised 355 patients with STEMI and CS (median age 68 years, 21% female, 6-month follow-up) to Impella® vs. standard care [ 7 ]. Of note, patients with CS due to mechanical complications, aortic valve disease, sepsis, pulmonary embolism (PE), right ventricular (RV) failure, prolonged shock > 24 h or Glasgow coma scale < 8 were excluded. At 6 months, Impella® was associated with a 26% reduction in the primary efficacy endpoint of all-cause mortality (45.8% vs. 58.5%, HR 0.74, 95% CI 0.55–0.99, p = 0.04) albeit at the cost of a marked increase in adverse events (a composite of severe bleeding, limb ischaemia, haemolysis, device failure, or worsening aortic regurgitation; 24.0% vs. 6.2%: HR 4.74, 95% CI 2.36–9.55), the long-term sequalae of which may nullify the early mortality benefit. It is unfortunate that recruitment into the Early Impella® Support in Patients With ST-Segment Elevation Myocardial Infarction Complicated by Cardiogenic Shock (RECOVER IV) study was terminated early (and perhaps prematurely) on the basis of DanGer Shock findings, since additional data may have helped clarify the risk/benefit for Impella® in CS [ 8 ]. In the modern era of PCI and secondary prevention, the role for beta-blockade in patients post MI with preserved LVEF has been queried. The Randomized Evaluation of Decreased Usage of Beta-Blockers After Acute Myocardial Infarction (REDUCE-AMI) study randomised 5200 patients post ACS with preserved LVEF (35% STEMI, median age 65 years; 22.5% female) to continuing beta-blockade vs. stopping beta-blocker. At median follow-up of 3.5 years, there was no difference in the primary endpoint of all-cause death or MI (7.9% vs. 8.3%; p = 0.64) and, interestingly, no difference in the rate of hospitalisation for bradyarrhythmia, hypotension, or syncope (3.4% vs. 3.2%; p = 0.64). However, the study was open-label and almost one fifth of patients crossed over groups at 1 year [ 9 ]. The Assessment of Beta-Blocker Interruption 1 Year After an Uncomplicated Myocardial Infarction on Safety and Symptomatic Cardiac Events Requiring Hospitalization (ABYSS) study randomised 3698 patients with previous MI (> 6 months, median 2.9 years), excluding those with chronic HF, LVEF < 40%, arrhythmia or hypertension (mean age 63.5 years; 82.8% male) to beta-blockade interruption or continuation. At median follow-up of 3.5 years, beta-blockade interruption failed to meet criteria for non-inferiority with a numerically higher composite primary outcome (death, MI, stroke or hospitalisation; 23.8% vs. 21.1%; p non-inferiority = 0.44) and failed to improve quality of life (QoL) scores (EQ-5D), but was associated with significant increase in blood pressure (BP) (+ 3.7/3.9 mmHg; p < 0.001) [ 10 ]. Results of further large RCTs such as DANBLOCK and BETAMI are thus eagerly awaited. Following on from the previously discussed TWILIGHT and STOP-DAPT trials which suggested a benefit for an abbreviated antiplatelet strategy [ 11 ], the Ticagrelor alone versus ticagrelor plus aspirin after percutaneous coronary intervention in patients with acute coronary syndromes (ULTIMATE-DAPT) study randomised 3400 patients who remained event-free 1 month after PCI for ACS (mean age 62 years, 74% male; 98.7% from China or Pakistan) to 1 month of DAPT then ticagrelor monotherapy vs. 12 months of DAPT (including ticagrelor). At 1 year, the abbreviated strategy was associated with a 55% reduction in the primary endpoint of clinically significant bleeding (BARC 2, 3 or 5; HR 0.45; 95% CI 0.30–0.66; p < 0.0001) but no difference in major adverse cardiovascular events and cerebrovascular events (MACCE) between groups (3.6% vs. 3.7%; p for non-inferiority < 0.0001) [ 12 ]. Valgimigli et al. undertook a meta-analysis of the six major DAPT de-escalation RCTs which evaluated ticagrelor monotherapy after abbreviated DAPT vs. standard care (23,256 patients; mean age 64 years; 23% female) [ 13 ]. At a median follow-up of 334 days, abbreviated DAPT was non-inferior for the primary endpoint of MACCE (2.8% vs. 3.2%: HR 0.91; 95% CI 0.78–1.07, p non-inferiority = 0.0039) and was associated with significantly lower bleeding in the intention-to-treat population (0.9% vs. 2.1% [HR 0.43, 95% CI 0.34–0.54; p < 0.0001 for superiority]). Previously discussed data from COLCOT and LoDoCo2 have suggested a role for colchicine for reduction of CV risk in both acute and chronic MI [ 14 ]. The Colchicine in Acute Myocardial Infarction (CLEAR SYNERGY) study randomised patients in a double-blind fashion ( n = 7062; mean age 61 years, 20% female) with acute MI (STEMI and NSTEMI) to low dose colchicine vs. placebo. At a median follow-up of 3 years, there was no difference in the primary outcome of major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE) (9.1% vs. 9.3%; HR 0.99 [95% CI 0.85–1.16, p = 0.93]) or in the secondary endpoints of death, MI or revascularisation [ 15 ]. While these results conflict with COLCOT and LoDoCo2, other small trials have reported possible harm such as increased rates of early left ventricular (LV) thrombus in COVERT-MI [ 10 ], thus ongoing studies are still warranted [ 11 ]. Advances in Percutaneous Coronary Intervention Drug-Coated Balloons Much interest surrounds the expanding role of drug-coated balloon (DCB) in PCI but their long-term efficacy and safety compared with drug-eluting stents (DES) has not been fully established. In the large Drug-coated balloon angioplasty with rescue stenting versus intended stenting for the treatment of patients with de novo coronary artery lesions (REC-CAGE-FREE-1) study, 2272 patients with de novo, non-complex coronary artery disease (CAD) were randomised to a paclitaxel DCB ( n = 1133) vs. sirolimus DES ( n = 1139). Of note, 9.4% of patients in the DCB arm required rescue DES after an unsatisfactory balloon angioplasty result. At 24 months, the DCB arm was associated with a higher incidence of the device-orientated composite endpoint (DoCE), which exceeded the pre-specified non-inferiority margin of 2.58% (6.4% vs. 3.4%; absolute difference 3.04%; p non-inferiority = 0.65) [ 16 ]. In contrast, a meta-analysis of four randomised studies ( n = 1145) comparing the long-term efficacy of DCB vs. DES in small-vessel coronary artery disease (CAD) reported that use of DCB was associated a numerical, albeit non-significant reduction in MACE at 3 years (18.5% vs. 24.5%, p = 0.27) [ 17 ]. Thus, while REC-CAGE-FREE-1 suggests that a conventional DES strategy remains the preferred strategy overall for de novo, non-complex CAD, in some patient subgroups, such as small-vessel CAD, a DCB strategy appears a reasonable alternative. The Agent Paclitaxel Coated PTCA Balloon Catheter for the Treatment of Subjects With In-Stent Restenosis (AGENT IDE) randomised trial examined the use of paclitaxel DCB as compared with uncoated balloon angioplasty (POBA) in patients with in-stent restenosis (ISR). AGENT IDE randomised patients with ISR (lesion length < 26 mm, reference vessel diameter 2–4 mm; recent STEMI excluded) to a DCB ( n = 406) vs. POBA ( n = 194) strategy. The DCB strategy was associated with a 41% reduction in the primary outcome of target lesion failure (TLF) occurred (17.9% vs. 28.6%; HR 0.59, 95% CI 0.42–0.84, p = 0.03) albeit with numerically higher cardiac death although this did not reach statistical significance (2.9% vs. 1.6%, p = 0.38). While the TLF results are encouraging the study was underpowered to evaluate cardiac mortality and thus ongoing studies are needed [ 18 ]. Use of a DCB to treat the side branch during a provisional stenting technique for coronary bifurcation is attractive as it may reduce the rates of later restenosis [ 19 ]. The Drug-coated balloon angioplasty of the side branch during provisional stenting: the multicentre randomized (DCB-BIF) trial randomised 784 patients undergoing a provisional strategy (vessel diameter > 2.5 mm, side branch > 70% ostial stenosis, length < 10 mm, severe calcific disease excluded) to DCB vs. non-compliant balloon (NCB) side branch dilatation [ 20 ]. Use of DCB was associated with a 44% reduction in the primary endpoint, a composite of cardiac death, target vessel MI (TV-MI) or target vessel revascularisation (TVR) at 1 year (7.2% vs. 12.5% [HR 0.56 (95% CI 0.35–0.88]) but interestingly this was driven by lower rate of target vessel MI (HR 0.50 [95%CI 0.30–0.84]) including spontaneous MI, but with no significant difference in target lesion revascularisation (1.3% vs. 1.5%; p = 1.00). Also of note, in the subgroup who underwent intravascular imaging optimisation (27.1%), no significant difference in the primary outcome was observed (HR 0.94 [95% CI 0.45–1.97]). Thus, while the overall results of DCB-BIF are encouraging, the lack of reduction in target lesion revascularisation (TLR) and diminution of benefit with imaging raise the possibility that the benefit could be related to the modified technique with additional prolonged (60 s) balloon dilatation, rather than the device itself. The DynamX bioadaptor (Elixer Medical) is a novel coronary implantable device deployed in a similar fashion to DES. DynamX provides a metal scaffold to maintain vessel dilatation at the time of PCI and delivers antiproliferative drug therapy but, unlike DES, its frame is joined together by a bioresorbable polymer which resorbs over 6 months and allows the helical cobalt-chromium strands of the bioadaptor to separate, restoring the native biomechanical motion of the vessel. The Elixir Bioadaptor vs. The Onyx Stent in De Novo Native Coronary Arteries (BIOADAPTOR) trial randomised 445 patients undergoing PCI for CCS to DynamX vs. Onyx. Clinical outcomes were non-inferior at 1 year [ 21 ]. At 2 years [ 21 ] DynamX was associated with a reduction in the primary outcome of TLF (a composite of cardiac death, TV-MI, ischaemia-driven TVR, or hospitalisation for angina) which did not reach statistical superiority in the pre-specified intention-to-treat analysis (2.3% vs. 5%; p = ns) although it did show benefit in per protocol analysis (1.9% vs. 5.5%; p = 0.046). The Percutaneous Coronary Intervention with a Bioadaptor Compared to a Contemporary Drug-Eluting Stent trial (INFINITY-SWEDEHEART) randomised 2399 patients undergoing PCI (including 77% with ACS) to DynamX vs. Onyx DES [ 22 ]. At 12 months, DynamX was non-inferior for the composite primary endpoint of CV death, MI or TLR (2.4% vs. 2.8%; p non-inferiority < 0.001) and in pre-specified analysis with events up to 6 months censored (the point at which the bioadaptor unlocks), DynamX was found to be superior to Onyx DES for the primary endpoint (0.3% vs. 1.7% p = 0.0079). While both studies are of interest, further research is needed to assess the long-term efficacy of this technology, particularly in more anatomically complex lesions. The optimal duration of DAPT in patients treated with DCB is unknown. Compared with DES, DCBs are associated with faster vessel healing and a reduced thrombotic burden which might merit shorter DAPT duration. The One-month DAPT followed by 5-month ticagrelor monotherapy in acute coronary syndromes with DCB (REC-CAGEGREE II) trial randomised 1948 patients in China with ACS undergoing PCI with paclitaxel-coated DCB (unstable angina 59%, small vessel disease 56%) to rapid de-escalation (ticagrelor-based DAPT for 1 month, then ticagrelor monotherapy for 5 months, then 6 months aspirin monotherapy) vs. standard ticagrelor-based DAPT for 12 months. Rapid de-escalation was non-inferior for the primary composite endpoint of death, MI, stroke, TLR or BARC 3 to 5 bleeding at 12 months (9% vs. 8.7%; p = 0.013 for non-inferiority), and showed a superior win ratio (1.43 [95% CI 1.12–1.83]) with fewer bleedings (0.5% vs. 1.7%; p = 0.018) but no significant difference in ischaemic endpoints [ 23 ]. While most patients post MI may still benefit from a year of DAPT, rapid de-escalation following DCB may be beneficial in those at high bleeding risk (HBR). Coronary Physiology A plethora of evidence supports deferral of a PCI in patients with non-flow-limiting coronary lesions [ 24 , 25 ]. However, it is known that ACS can be caused by non-flow-limiting, lipid-rich coronary plaques also known as ‘vulnerable plaques’. Vulnerable plaques can be characterised at the time of PCI using intracoronary imaging techniques such as intravascular ultrasound (IVUS) and optical coherence tomography (OCT). The Preventive percutaneous coronary intervention versus optimal medical therapy alone for the treatment of vulnerable atherosclerotic coronary plaques (PREVENT) trial randomised 1606 patients in centres across South Korea with non-flow-limiting vulnerable plaques identified at the time of angiography to PCI plus OMT versus OMT alone. Patients included needed to have a coronary stenosis > 50% with a negative fractional flow reserve (FFR < 0.8) and two of the following: (i) minimal lumen area (MLA) < 4.0 mm 2 , (ii) plaque burden > 70%, (iii) thin cap fibroatheroma by OCT or IVUS, (iv) lipid-rich plaque by near-infrared spectroscopy ( max LCBI 4mm > 315). Most patients (84%) were stable presentations. The primary endpoint of TLF at 2 years for PCI + OMT vs. OMT alone was 0.4% vs. 3.4% ( p = 0.0003). In long-term follow-up conducted at 7 years TLF was 6.5% in the PCI group versus 9.4% in the OMT only group ( p = 0.0097). Whilst all cause death at 2 years was numerically lower (0.5% versus 1.3%) in those undergoing PCI this did not achieve statistical significance ( p > 0.05), and the primary outcome was predominantly driven by a lower rate of TLR (0.1% versus 2.4%) in the PCI group ( p = 0.0001). The PREVENT trial demonstrated that in the subpopulation of patients with non-flow-limiting vulnerable plaques a preventative strategy incorporating PCI was superior to OMT with respect to TLF [ 26 ]. Given the low event rate in both groups, further research is needed in predominantly ACS populations to evaluate whether non-culprit vessel, non-flow-limiting vulnerable plaques should be treated at the time of culprit vessel PCI. Functional assessment of intermediate diameter stenoses during invasive coronary angiography (ICA) with fractional flow reserve (FFR) or instantaneous wave free ratio (iFR) is recommended to guide decision to revascularise [ 1 ]. Quantitative flow ratio (QFR) estimates FFR using automated computer analysis of angiographic images and therefore might obviate the need for pressure wire assessment. Previous randomised study comparing QFR-guided PCI to angiography only guided PCI demonstrated a reduction in the rate of MI or TLR in the QFR-guided strategy [ 11 ] and has led to its endorsement in international guidelines. The Quantitative flow ratio versus fractional flow reserve for coronary revascularisation guidance (FAVOR III Europe) trial was a randomised, open-label, non-inferiority trial of 2000 patients with CCS or stabilised ACS with at least one intermediate-diameter, non-culprit stenosis allocated to an FFR-guided revascularisation strategy versus QFR-guided revascularisation strategy. Patients with complex disease, including LMS, severe tortuosity or chronic total occlusions (CTO) were excluded. The cut-off used for functional significance was < 0.8 for both the FFR and QFR groups. The primary outcome measure was a composite of death, MI and unplanned revascularisation at 12 months with a pre-defined non-inferiority margin set at 3.4%. At 12 months the primary outcome occurred in 6.7% of the QFR group versus 4.2% in the FFR group [HR 1.63 (95% CI 1.11–2.41)] and failed to meet the threshold of statistical significance for the pre-specified non-inferiority margin. The main driver of the difference in the primary outcome was the higher rate of MI in the QFR group 3.7% versus 2% in the FFR group (HR 1.84 (95% CI 1.07–3.17)]. The median QFR value was 0.81 compared with 0.84 in those undergoing FFR resulting in a 21% increase in the number of revascularisation procedures in the QFR group. There was more fluoroscopy time in the FFR arm, but no difference in the procedure duration or contrast volume administered [ 27 ]. FAVOR III Europe demonstrates that invasive FFR assessment of intermediate diameter stenoses remains the optimum strategy for guiding revascularisation and should be used in preference to QFR where both strategies are available. Previous studies have demonstrated that patients with suspected angina and unobstructed coronary arteries may have better symptoms control if treatment is guided by indices of coronary microvascular dysfunction rather than angiography alone [ 28 ]. However, real-world prevalence of microvascular dysfunction using a stepwise protocol has yet to be prospectively evaluated. In Combining angiography and pre-specified intracoronary testing in patients with chronic coronary syndromes (AID-angio), 314 prospective patients with suspected CCS undergoing ICA had FFR of all focal stenoses and, if FFR negative, had further evaluation by coronary flow reserve (CFR), index microcirculatory resistance (IMR) and acetylcholine testing. Use of coronary physiology (which added a median of only 15 min to each case) was associated with a marked increase in diagnosis of ischaemia compared with the treating cardiologist’s opinion after ICA alone (84.2% vs. 32.2% p < 0.001) resulting in management change in 59.9% of cases. Serious adverse events occurred in 0.9% of cases [ 29 ]. The AID-angio study suggests that the prevalence of ischaemia with normal coronary arteries (INOCA) may be significantly underestimated in a real-world population with suspected CCS. Non-invasive Coronary Physiology Artificial intelligence derived quantitative coronary technology (AI QCT) may improve cardiac computed tomography (CT) assessment by better evaluating the burden of atherosclerosis throughout the entire coronary vascular tree, including the degree of non-calcific plaque. The Quantitative COroNary CT Angiography Evaluation For Evaluation of Clinical Outcomes: An InteRnational , Multicenter Registry (CONFIRM2) studied 3551 patients with suspected CAD referred for CT coronary angiography (CTCA). AI-QCT was used to generate 24 measures of atherosclerosis burden including parameters related to plaque volume, CAD extent, AI-identified high-risk plaques and diameter stenosis [ 30 ]. During a median of 4.7 years follow-up, AI-QCT was more predictive of adverse outcomes than Diamond–Forrester pre-test probability (PTP) with diameter stenosis and the noncalcified plaque volume the most predictive of MACE and of additive value to Diamond and Forrester PTP plus other risk factors. While these data are interesting, further research is needed to assess whether treatment guided by AI-generated risk estimation leads to improved outcome. Intravascular Imaging and Calcium Modification In the OCT vs. angiography for guidance of calcified lesions PCI (CALIPSO) study, 134 patients with clinically stable, moderate to-severe calcific coronary artery disease were randomised to a pre-defined OCT-guided lesion preparation and stent optimisation vs. an angiography-guided strategy. All 134 patients underwent an OCT run at the end of the case. OCT guidance was associated with a larger final minimum stent area (MSA) (6.5 vs. 5.0 mm 2 ; p < 0.001) and greater use of advanced lesion modification techniques (63 vs. 37%; p = 0.002), mostly commonly intravascular lithotripsy (IVL) [ 31 ]. The CALIPSO study thus supports use of OCT guidance in the setting of coronary calcium. Whether optimising such parameters improves clinical outcomes was assessed in the Optical coherence tomography-guided versus angiography-guided percutaneous coronary intervention for patients with complex lesions (OCCUPI) trial which randomised 1604 patients in South Korea undergoing complex PCI to OCT guidance vs. angiography alone. OCT guidance was associated with a greater post-procedure minimal lumen diameter (MLD) (2.75 vs. 2.59 mm, p < 0.001), and at 1 year, a 38% reduction in the composite of cardiac death, MI, stent thrombosis or TVR (4.6% vs. 7.4%; HR 0.62; 95% CI 0.41–0.93), predominantly driven by fewer spontaneous MI (0.9% vs. 2.4%; HR 0.36; 95% CI 0.15–0.86) [ 32 ]. The clinical value of coronary atherectomy for calcified lesion preparation was assessed in the Evaluation of Treatment Strategies for Severe CaLcIfic Coronary Arteries: Orbital Atherectomy vs. Conventional Angioplasty Technique Prior to Implantation of Drug-Eluting StEnts (ECLIPSE study) which randomised 2005 patients with severely calcific angiographic CAD to atherectomy vs. balloon-only preparation prior to DES. The use of IVL was prohibited. Intravascular imaging was performed in 62% of patients in both study arms. Atherectomy was associated with numerically greater post-PCI MSA (7.44 vs. 7.05 mm 2 ; p = 0.08) in the OCT subgroup ( n = 555) but no improvement in TVF at 1 year (11.5% vs. 10%; p = 0.28), higher contrast volume (179.4 ml vs. 160 ml; p < 0.001), longer duration (73.2 min vs. 60.1 min; p < 0.001), and more frequent slow flow at the end of the procedure (1.4% vs. 0.4%; p = 0.03) [ 33 ]. This disappointing result highlights the ongoing clinical value of careful image-guided balloon preparation. Novel Antianginal Devices The coronary sinus reducer (CSR), a stainless-steel mesh percutaneously inserted into the coronary sinus, has a class IIb recommendation for treatment of refractory angina by diverting blood flow from more perfused to less perfused areas of myocardium [ 1 ]. The Coronary sinus reducer for the treatment of refractory angina (ORBITA-COSMIC) trial randomised 51 patients with refractory angina and epicardial coronary disease but no revascularisation option to CSR vs. sham control [ 34 ]. Key exclusion criteria were right atrial pressure > 15 mmHg, severe LV systolic failure, permanent pacemaker or indication for cardiac resynchronisation therapy (CRT). Device embolisation occurred in two patients. At 6 months, CSR did not improve the primary endpoint of stress myocardial blood flow in ischaemic segments (0.06 ml/min/g between group difference [95% CI − 0.09 to 0.2 ml/min/g]) but was associated with a 40% reduction in the co-primary endpoint of daily anginal episodes although the reason for this, given the lack of blood flow improvement, remains unclear. Periprocedural Care European Society of Anaesthesiology (ESA) guidelines recommend patients do not consume solids for 6 h and liquids for 2 h prior to invasive procedures requiring conscious sedation [ 1 ]. In the Safety and Care OF no Fasting prior to catheterization laboratory procedures (SCOFF) trial 716 patients across six sites in Australia undergoing coronary angiography or cardiac implantable electronic device (CIED) were randomised to no fasting vs. standard fasting. Of note, 27% had diabetes mellitus and 57% had a diagnostic only procedure. No fasting was associated with a 37% reduction in the primary composite outcome of aspiration pneumonia, hypotension, hypoglycaemia or hyperglycaemia (12.0% vs. 19.1% meeting both non-inferiority and superiority criteria), mainly driven by lower rates of hypotension and hyperglycaemia. No aspiration pneumonias occurred in either group [ 35 ]. The SCOFF trial suggests a non-fasting strategy prior to catheter laboratory procedures may be preferable although complex patients more likely to experience nausea or vomiting may have been under-represented. Hybrid Revascularisation Hybrid coronary revascularisation (HCR) combines minimally invasive left internal mammary artery (LIMA) grafting to the left anterior descending (LAD) artery with PCI to non-LAD disease. The Prospective randomised pilOt study evaLuating the safety and efficacy of hybrid revascularisation in MultI-vessel coronary artery DisEaSe (POLMIDES) randomised 200 patients with MV-CAD from a single centre to HCR vs. conventional CABG, and previously reported a numerical, non-significant reduction in the primary endpoint of all-cause mortality at 5 years (6.4% vs. 9.2%; p = 0.69) [ 35 ]. New 10-year follow-up data available for 95.9% of HCR and 95.1% of conventional CABG patients reported a 43% reduction in the all-cause mortality (20.4% vs. 36.3%; p = 0.02) with curves diverging at 8 years of follow-up [ 36 ]. Numerical but non-significant reduction was noted for MI (13.3% vs. 16.7%), coronary reintervention (45.9% vs. 51.0%), stroke (5.9% vs. 7.1%), and MACCE (56.1% vs. 68.6%). While POLMIDES findings are encouraging, larger multicentre studies are required to evaluate more definitively. Advances in Structural Interventions Transcatheter Aortic Valve Interventions (TAVI) The role of TAVI in low-risk patients with severe aortic stenosis (AS) has been established in the previous trials such as EVOLUT low risk and PARTNER 3 [ 6 , 37 ]. The investigator-initiated Randomized Trial of TAVI vs. SAVR in Patients With Severe Aortic Valve Stenosis at Low to Intermediate Risk of Mortality (DEDICATE) study compared TAVI vs. SAVR in low to intermediate surgical risk patients ( n = 1414: mean age 74 ± 4 years; 57% male, median STS 1.8%) with operator discretion permitted regarding TAVI valve selection. At 1 year, TAVI vs. SAVR met non-inferiority criteria and indeed was associated with a numerical 47% reduction in the primary outcome of death or stroke (5.4% vs. 10.0%), 57% reduction in death (2.6% vs. 6.2%) and 39% reduction in stroke (2.9% vs. 4.7%) respectively [ 38 ]. The magnitude of difference was surprising and appeared mainly due to higher than expected event rates in the SAVR group (possibly influenced by the COVID pandemic), although better individualized valve sizing in TAVI may have been of benefit. Whether the value of TAVI in low-risk patients extends to those with bicuspid pathology is uncertain. The Nordic Aortic Valve Intervention (NOTION) 2 trial randomised low-risk patients ≤ 75 years with severe symptomatic AS ( n = 370, of whom 100 had bicuspid valves; mean age 71.1 years, median STS 1.1%) to TAVI or SAVR. While, in the overall cohort, TAVI appeared similar to SAVR regarding the primary endpoint of death, stroke, or rehospitalisation (10.2% vs. 7.1%, HR 1.4; 95% CI 0.7–2.9; p = 0.3), in the bicuspid subgroup, TAVI was associated with a higher primary endpoint incidence (14.3% vs. 3.9%, HR 3.8; 95% CI 0.8–18.5) [ 39 ] highlighting the need for further research in this subgroup. Long-term outcomes in patients undergoing TAVI vs. SAVR were assessed by Talanas et al. in a meta-analysis of seven recent landmark RCTs ( n = 7785) [ 40 ]. At a mean follow-up of 5.76 ± 0.073 years there was no difference in the primary endpoint of death or disabling stroke (HR 1.02; 95% CI 0.93–1.11), valve thrombosis (0.68% vs. 0.23%: RR 2.52, p = 0.47) but TAVI was associated with a higher rate of PPM insertion (HR 1.98; 95% CI 1.34–2.91; p < 0.01), particularly if using a self-expanding valves (SEV) (HR 2.83; 95% CI 2.27–3.54). Much interest surrounds the timing of aortic valve intervention with recent data from RECOVERY and AVATAR supporting earlier surgical intervention in severe asymptomatic AS [ 11 ]. The Evaluation of TAVR Compared to Surveillance for Patients With Asymptomatic Severe Aortic Stenosis (EARLY TAVR) randomised asymptomatic patients ( n = 901: mean age 76 years, 29% female, mean STS 1.8%) with severe AS, and a negative treadmill test, to early intervention with TAVI vs. ongoing surveillance. At a median follow-up of 3.8 years, early TAVI was associated with a marked reduction in the primary outcome (death, stroke or unplanned CV hospitalisation; 26.8% vs. 45.3%; p < 0.001) although this was primarily driven by lower rates of hospitalisation (often due to natural progression in AS requiring intervention) but no significant difference in death or stroke [ 41 ]. The Early Valve Replacement Guided by Biomarkers of Left Ventricular Decompensation in Asymptomatic Patients With Severe Aortic Stenosis (EVOLVED) study evaluated mid-wall cardiac fibrosis on cardiac magnetic resonance imaging (CMR) (suggesting the left ventricle may be starting to decompensate) as an indicator for early intervention. In a 1:1 open-label randomisation, patients ( n = 224; mean age 73 year; 28% female) with asymptomatic severe AS and mid-wall scarring were randomized to valve intervention (SAVR or TAVI) vs. conservative management. At 1-year follow-up, early intervention (mainly by SAVR) was not associated with reduction in the primary composite outcome (death or unplanned hospitalisation; 18% vs. 23%: HR 0.79 [95% CI 0.44–1.43; p = 0.44]) but reductions in secondary endpoints of New York Heart Association (NYHA) class and aortic stenosis-related hospitalisation were noted [ 42 ]. While these secondary findings are only hypothesis-generating, they are in keeping with EARLY TAVR data and suggest there may be merit in a larger study testing CMR and/or other biomarkers to guide earlier intervention, ideally by TAVI. Patients with concomitant HF with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF) and moderate AS may theoretically benefit from earlier intervention to reduce risk of further decompensation although supporting data are limited. The Transcatheter Aortic Valve Replacement to UNload the Left Ventricle in patients with ADvanced heart failure (TAVR UNLOAD) study randomised patients ( n = 178: mean age 77 years, 21% female) with symptomatic HFrEF and moderate AS to TAVI vs. continued surveillance. Patients with acutely decompensated HF were excluded. TAVI was not associated with a significant improvement in the primary endpoint of death, stroke, HF hospitalisation and change in Kansas City Cardiomyopathy Questionnaire (KCCQ) at a median follow-up of 23 months (47.6% vs. 36.6%; win ratio 1.31 but p = 0.14) but was associated with greater improvement in the secondary endpoint of quality of life (KCCQ) at 1 year ( p = 0.018) [ 43 ]. Thus, TAVI cannot be routinely recommended for this patient group at present although two larger, ongoing trials—EXPAND II and PROGRESS—may give additional clarity. The role of cerebral embolic protection (CEP) in TAVI remains controversial with data from the previously discussed PROTECTED TAVR study demonstrating no reduction in stroke risk for routine CEP use [ 37 ]. A subsequent exploratory subgroup analysis reported that routine CEP use in US patients appeared to be associated with lower rates of stroke (1.3% vs. 2.6%; p = 0.045) whereas no difference was seen in non-US patients (3.7% vs. 3.3%; p = 0.662) [ 44 ]. This variance may be due to differences in stroke diagnosis by region but further robust data from the ongoing BHF PROTECT study are eagerly awaited. Stable CAD may be present in up to half of TAVI patients, but the optimal CAD treatment strategy remains unclear. The Third Nordic Aortic Valve Intervention (NOTION 3) study randomised 455 patients undergoing TAVI (mean age 82 years; 32% female, median SYNTAX 9, median LVEF 60%) with severe symptomatic AS and obstructive CAD (vessel size ≥ 2.5 mm and FFR ≤ 0.80 or angiographic stenosis ≥ 90%) to PCI vs. conservative strategy. In the PCI group, complete revascularisation was achieved in 89% and 74% of patients had PCI prior to TAVI. At 2 years, PCI was associated with a significant reduction in the primary outcome of death, MI or urgent revascularisation (26% vs. 36%: HR 0.71; 95% CI 0.51–0.99, p = 0.04), no difference in death alone, but significant reduction in MI (14.0% vs. 7.0%; HR 0.54; 95% CI 0.30–0.97). In subgroup analysis, PCI appeared particularly beneficial for patients < 82 years (HR 0.56: 95% CI 0.33–0.95) and those with visually severe (> 90%) angiographic stenosis (95% CI 0.34–0.82) [ 45 ]. Coronary artery obstruction (CAO) following TAVI is a rare but devastating complication, with patients undergoing re-do procedures being at particularly high risk. Novel techniques such as BASILICA have been developed for treating this cohort but these are associated with a significant operator learning curve [ 46 ]. The Open-Label Clinical Study to Demonstrate the Safety and Effectiveness of the ShortCut™ Device (ShortCut) study prospectively enrolled patients ( n = 60; 77.0 ± 9.6 years; 70% female) with failed bioprosthetic aortic valves, at high risk of CAO, undergoing TAVI study to undergo treatment ShortCut (PiCardia), a novel dedicated leaflet modification device. Successful leaflet splitting was achieved in all 60 patients with absence of the primary safety endpoint (procedure related mortality or stroke at 7 days) achieved in 59 patients (Fig. 1 ). Importantly, at 30 days, 95% patients were free of CAO [ 47 ]. Further data in larger populations are eagerly awaited. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Image of the Pi-Cardia ShortCut™ device, a novel dedicated transcatheter device designed to modify aortic valve leaflets by mechanical splitting [ 47 ]. Image supplied with permission from Pi-Cardia Conduction disturbance requiring permanent pacemaker implantation is a recognised complication of TAVI but some studies suggest pacemaker dependence may resolve over time. Song et al. prospectively enrolled 70 patients (mean age 74.3 years; 41.4% women) with conduction disturbance post TAVI for temporary pacemaker insertion (high-degree AVB n = 60, first-degree block n = 10). At 1 month, only 14% were still pacing dependent but 76% ( n = 53) were deemed to no longer require permanent pacing. Independent predictors of need for permanent pacing were longer baseline PR interval and a membranous septum length minus implant depth [ΔMSID] ≥ 3 mm [ 48 ]. Given the trend of rapid discharge post TAVR, this protocol may provide a means of safely discharging patients with conduction disease without committing them to lifetime PPM insertion but longer follow-up data are needed. Female patients commonly have smaller aortic annuli which can lead to suboptimal results or complications during TAVI. The Small Annuli Randomized to Evolut or SAPIEN Trial (SMART) randomised 621 female patients (mean age 80.2 years) to SEV (Evolut PRO/PRO+/FX, Medtronic) vs. a balloon expandable valve (BEV) (SAPIEN 3/3 Ultra, Edwards Lifesciences). At 12 months, SEV did not reduce the co-primary clinical endpoint (death, disabling stroke, or heart failure-related rehospitalisation; 9.4% vs. 11.8%; p = 0.35) but was associated with reduction in the co-primary valve endpoint of bioprosthetic valve dysfunction (8.4% vs. 41.8%; p < 0.001), lower rates of PPM, improved mean aortic valve gradient reduction and higher effective orifice area [ 49 ]. These data highlight that, in female patients with small annuli, TAVR with SEV may be preferable to BEV. Mitral and Tricuspid Intervention The role of transcatheter TV therapies is rapidly expanding [ 50 ]. The Multicenter, Randomized, Controlled Study to Assess Mitral vAlve reconsTrucTion for advancEd Insufficiency of Functional or iscHemic ORigiN (MATTERHORN) study randomised 208 patients (mean age 70.5 years; 40% female; 86% NYHA class III or IV; LVEF > 20% [mean 43%]; mean EUROSCORE II 3%) with severe secondary mitral regurgitation (MR) to mitral transcatheter edge-to-edge repair (M-TEER) (MitraClip, abbott) vs. surgical mitral valve (MV) repair. M-TEER met non-inferiority criteria for the primary composite efficacy outcome at 1 year (death, heart failure hospitalisation, mitral re-intervention, assist device implantation, or stroke; 16.7% vs. 22.5% [ p non-inferiority < 0.001]) and was associated with reduction in the primary composite safety endpoint at 30 days (14.9% vs. 54.8%: 95% CI − 51 to − 27; p < 0.001) [ 51 ]. While an encouraging result, the study was limited by short follow-up making full comparison of the two modalities difficult and failed to include a medical therapy arm (of relevance given that this cohort are traditionally managed medically). The utility of transcatheter MV repair in patients with HF remains unclear. The Clinical Evaluation of the Safety and Effectiveness of the MitraClip System in the Treatment of Clinically Significant Functional Mitral Regurgitation (RESHAPE HF2) study randomised 505 patients (grade 3–4 + MR, recent HF admission or increased BNP, and symptoms despite maximally tolerated medical therapy) to M-TEER with MitraClip plus medical therapy vs. medial therapy alone [ 52 ]. At 2 years, M-TEER was associated with a 36% reduction in the primary endpoint (CV death or first/recurrent HF hospitalisation rate); 37 vs. 58.9 events/100 patient-years; RR 0.64: 95% CI 0.48–0.85, p = 0.002) mainly due to reduction in HF hospitalisation. The study was limited by change of inclusion criteria and endpoints mid-trial and somewhat out-of-date medical therapy. The Transcatheter Valve Replacement in Severe Tricuspid Regurgitation (TRISCEND II) study randomised patients with severe symptomatic tricuspid regurgitation (TR) (mean age 79 years, 76% female) to EVOQUE transcatheter tricuspid valve replacement (TTVR) ( n = 267) vs. control ( n = 133). Dramatic improvements in TR were noted, with < 5% having residual ≥ moderate TR at 1 year. At 1 year, TTVR was associated with improvement in the composite primary endpoint (death, need for RV assist device/transplant, percutaneous TV intervention, HF hospitalisation, KCCQ-OS > 10 points, NYHA > 1 class and 6-min walk distance > 30 m) with a win ratio of 2.02 (95% CI 1.56–2.62, p < 0.0001) driven primarily by improved symptoms and quality of life (but no difference in death or HF hospitalisation) [ 53 ]. TTVR was associated with marked improvement in tricuspid valve regurgitation at 1 year, but increased risk of severe bleeding (15.4% vs. 5.3%; p = 0.003) or need for pacemaker (17.4% vs. 2.3%; p < 0.001).The previously discussed, Trial to Evaluate Treatment With Abbott Transcatheter Clip Repair System in Patients With Moderate or Greater Tricuspid Regurgitation (TRILLUMINATE) study [ 37 ] published new follow-up data with TriClip (Abbott) vs. medical therapy in patients with symptomatic severe TR ( n = 572; mean age 78.1 ± 7.8 years, 58.9% female). At 1 year the primary hierarchal composite endpoint (death, tricuspid valve surgery, HF hospitalisation, and improvement in KCCQ) favoured TEER vs. medical therapy alone (win ratio 1.84; 95% CI 1.40–2.45, p < 0.0001). This was largely driven by an improvement in QoL metrics but with no difference in mortality or HF hospitalisations [ 54 ]. Advances in Electrophysiology and Devices Atrial Fibrillation Persisting concerns regarding a possible placebo effect of pulmonary vein isolation (PVI) in patients with symptomatic paroxysmal or persistent atrial fibrillation (AF) were evaluated in the Randomized Sham-controlled Study of Pulmonary Vein Isolation in Symptomatic Atrial Fibrillation (SHAM-PVI) [ 55 ], which randomised 126 patients with medical therapy failure/intolerance to PVI with cryoablation vs. sham with phrenic nerve pacing. PVI was associated with significantly greater reduction from baseline in AF burden at 6 months (excluding the 3-month blanking period) detected by continuous monitoring (60.31% vs. 35%; 95% CI 0.15–0.42; p < 0.001) with benefit in both paroxysmal and persistent subgroups, and higher quality of life scores. In summary, while a placebo effect (35%) was confirmed, SHAM-PVI confirmed PVI was associated with significant additional objective and symptomatic benefits in appropriate patients. The ADVENT trial previously demonstrated non-inferiority of pulsed-field ablation (PFA) vs. conventional thermal catheter ablation for paroxysmal AF [ 6 ]. However, PFA’s greater myocardial tissue selectivity could theoretically be a disadvantage due to reduced autonomic denervation (since autonomic denervation may reduce long-term AF burden). Sub-analysis of 379 patients (mean age 63 years, 39% female) randomised in the ADVENT trial to PFA ( n = 194) vs. thermal ablation ( n = 185) compared heart rate (HR; average, min, max) and heart rate variability (HRV) at baseline, 6 and 12 months using 72-h Holter monitoring. Despite potentially lower autonomic denervation, PFA actually showed stable HR at baseline, 6 and 12 months (67.7 vs. 68.6 vs. 68.1 bpm; p = 0.4696) whereas thermal ablation was associated with an increase in HR over time (67.3 vs. 72.9 vs. 71.6 bpm; p < 0.01) with poorer HRV vs PFA ( p < 0.01) [ 56 ]. Turagam et al. evaluated the safety and efficacy of a novel spherical PFA catheter (Kardium Inc, Vancouver, Canada), with the ability to map and ablate in a ‘single-shot’ in 48 patients with paroxysmal ( n = 23) or permanent ( n = 25) AF in a single centre. Primary efficacy endpoint of complete electrical PVI was achieved in 100% of pulmonary veins and of the 89.5% of patients who underwent invasive remapping at 3 months, 93.5% of PVs remained isolated. Additionally, freedom from atrial arrhythmia at 12 months (following a 90-day blanking period) in the paroxysmal and permanent AF groups was 84.2% and 80% respectively [ 57 ]. Although results are promising, larger randomised studies with continuous cardiac monitoring are required. The Treatment of Persistent Atrial Fibrillation With Sphere-9 Catheter and Affera Mapping and Ablation System (SPHERE PER-AF) study randomised 420 patients with persistent AF to treatment with a novel Sphere-9™ system catheter (Medtronic Affera) which integrates PFA plus RFA with high density mapping vs. conventional contact force sensing RFA. At 1 year, Sphere-9 met non-inferiority criteria for both efficacy (73.5% vs. 65.2%; p < 0.0001 for non-inferiority) and safety (serious procedure or device-related events; 1.4% vs. 1%; p < 0.0001 for non-inferiority), and was associated with reduced skin-to-skin procedure time (100.9 ± 30.8 vs. 126.1 ± 49.2 min) and reduced energy application time (7.1 ± 2.0 vs. 36.0 ± 17.9 min) [ 58 ]. The improved procedural efficiency is encouraging and further assessment is merited. The previously discussed Multi-national survey on the methods, efficacy, and safety on the post-approval clinical use of pulsed field ablation (MANIFEST-PF) demonstrated good clinical effectiveness with rare safety events in 1758 patients with AF who underwent PFA [ 6 ]. MANIFEST-17k, reported PFA safety in 178,642 consecutive unselected patients from 106 centres (mean age 64 years, 34.7% female, paroxysmal AF 57.8%, permanent AF 35.2%, long-standing permanent AF 5.6%). Major and minor complication rates were low at 0.98% and 3.21% respectively including zero oesophageal complications, zero persistent phrenic nerve injury and zero symptomatic pulmonary vein stenoses. Overall mortality rate was low at 0.03% [ 59 ]. These results demonstrate an excellent safety profile with preferential myocardial tissue ablation across the largest cohort of PFA patients to date. Artificial intelligence (AI) may be important in offering personalised therapies to patients with atrial fibrillation. The Tailored vs. Anatomical Ablation Strategy for Persistent Atrial Fibrillation (TAILORED-AF) trial randomised 374 patients with persistent AF (mean age 66 years, 21% female, mean LVEF 55%) to AI-directed ablation in addition to PVI (tailored arm) vs. conventional PVI (anatomical arm). AI-tailored treatment led to longer procedural times (178 vs. 92 min; p < 0.001) but at 12 months follow-up (in n = 174 vs. 174) was associated with greater freedom from AF (88% vs. 70%, p < 0.0001) [ 60 ]. Long-standing persistent atrial fibrillation (LSPAF) is challenging to treat because of atrial remodelling and neurohormonal activation. Previous non-randomised studies have suggested superiority of surgical ablation (SA) vs. catheter ablation (CA). The Catheter Ablation Versus Thoracoscopic Surgical Ablation in Long Standing Persistent Atrial Fibrillation (CASA-AF) trial randomised 115 patients with LSPAF (≥ 12 months) to CA vs. SA [ 61 ]. At 36 months (available in n = 104; 90.4%) there was no significant difference between the CA and SA groups in freedom from implantable loop recorder-defined atrial arrhythmia ≥ 30 s, ≥ 75% arrhythmia burden reduction, questionnaire quality of life scores or need for repeat procedures. Given the reduced cost of CA (− 37% vs. SA), CA may be the preferred first-line treatment. AF often co-exists with stable coronary artery disease but the optimum anti-thrombotic strategy in these patients has been unclear. The Edoxaban Versus Edoxaban With antiPlatelet Agent In Patients With Atrial Fibrillation and Chronic Stable Coronary Artery Disease (EPIC-CAD) randomised 1060 patients (mean age 72, 23% female) to edoxaban monotherapy ( n = 524) vs. edoxaban plus a single antiplatelet agent ( n = 516) [ 62 ]. Edoxaban monotherapy was associated with a 56% reduction in the primary composite endpoint (death, myocardial infarction, stroke, systemic embolism, unplanned urgent revascularisation, major bleeding or clinically relevant non-major bleeding; 6.8% vs. 16.2%, HR 0.44, 95% CI 0.3–0.65, p < 0.001) which was driven by a marked reduction in major or clinically relevant bleeding (4.7% vs. 14.2%, HR 0.34; 95% CI 0.22–0.53) without any excess in ischaemic events. These findings are consistent with the Atrial Fibrillation and Ischemic Events with Rivaroxaban in Patients with Stable Coronary Artery Disease (AFIRE) study, which showed superior safety for rivaroxaban monotherapy vs. combination therapy [ 63 ]. Anticoagulation with warfarin is recommended for at least 3 months following bioprosthetic valve implantation in patients both with AF (class I) and sinus rhythm (class IIa) [ 64 ]. Izumi randomised 410 patients (known AF in approximately 20%) undergoing aortic or mitral bioprosthetic valve replacement to a minimum of 12 weeks of edoxaban vs. warfarin therapy [ 65 ]. Edoxaban was associated with a similar incidence of the primary endpoint of stroke or systemic embolisation (0.5% vs. 1.5%), numerically higher bleeding rate (4.1% vs. 1.0%) but no fatal or intracranial haemorrhage. One fatal intracranial haemorrhage occurred with warfarin. While results are encouraging and in keeping with previous Korean data, the low event rates make definitive comparison difficult. AF may increase the risk of cognitive impairment. Kühne compared standardised brain imaging and Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) in 1732 patients with AF from the Swiss-AF trial (mean age 72 years, 27% female) vs. an extension cohort of 963 patients without AF (mean age 74 years, 37% female) [ 66 ]. Following risk factor adjustment including oral anticoagulation, patients with AF were more likely to have brain infarct (OR 1.72; 95% CI 1.28–2.33) including clinically silent infarct (OR 1.87; 95% CI 1.31–2.65) and scored 1 point lower on MoCA. This study was limited by the lack of continuous rhythm monitoring in the non-AF group and uncertainty regarding the infarct timing. The Blinded Randomised trial of Anticoagulation to prevent Ischaemic stroke and Neurocognitive impairment in Atrial Fibrillation (BRAIN-AF) randomised 1235 patients (mean age 53 years, 26% female) with non-valvular AF (paroxysmal 78.5%, persistent 11.2%, permanent 10.4%) at low risk of stroke to rivaroxaban vs. placebo. Disappointingly, rivaroxaban did not reduce the primary composite endpoint (≥ 3 point reduction in MoCA score, stroke or TIA; 7% vs. 6.4%; p = 0.46) [ 67 ] perhaps explained by cognitive decline accounting for 91% of the primary endpoint and annualised stroke only 0.8%. Left atrial appendage closure (LAAC) offers a mechanical option for stroke risk reduction in AF, but data is lacking regarding LAAC following ablation treatment. In the Comparison of Anticoagulation With Left Atrial Appendage Closure After Atrial Fibrillation Ablation (OPTION) trial, 1600 patients (mean age 69.6 years, 34.1% female) with AF and high stroke risk (CHA2DS2-VASc ≥ 2 male patients, ≥ 3 female patients) were randomised to either ablation plus LAAC vs. ablation plus oral anticoagulation and followed for 36 months. Ablation plus LAAC was associated with significant reduction in the primary safety endpoint (major bleeding or clinically relevant non-major bleeding; 8.5% vs. 18.1%; p < 0.001 for superiority) and met non-inferiority for the primary efficacy endpoint (death, stroke and systemic embolisation; 5.3% vs. 5.8%; p < 0.001 for non-inferiority) [ 68 ]. LAAC may thus have an evolving role in high bleeding risk patients. Stroke prevention and rhythm control Therapy: Evaluation of an Educational Programme of the European society of cardiology in a cluster-Randomised trial in patients with Atrial Fibrillation (STEEER-AF) randomised 195 clinicians looking after 1732 patients with AF (mean age 69 years, 37% female; mean CHA 2 DSVAS 2 C 3.2) [ 69 ] to a 16-week education course vs. control (usual care). Education was associated with an increase in adherence to rhythm control guidelines (33.9% vs. 22.9%; p = 0.03), but no significant difference regarding stroke prevention. Genomic evidence suggests that metabolic stress is implicated in the pathophysiology of AF. The Targeting Risk Interventions and Metformin for Atrial Fibrillation (TRIM-AF) trial randomised 149 patients (mean age 74, 39% female) with AF and a cardiac device (allowing continuous rhythm monitoring) to standard of care (written information on lifestyle modifications) vs. metformin vs. lifestyle and risk factor management (LRFM) vs. metformin plus LRFM [ 70 ]. Relative change in AF burden from baseline to 9–12 months was − 73.5%, − 85.9%, − 48.9% and − 72.4% respectively. Of note, at 12 months, half of the patients in the metformin arm and one quarter in the metformin plus LRFM had discontinued the medication. The authors concluded that simply providing written literature can have an important impact on outcomes. Ventricular Tachycardia CA for ventricular tachycardia (VT) in patients with ischaemic cardiomyopathy is most often reserved for those who fail drug therapy. The Ventricular Tachycardia Antiarrhythmics or Ablation in Ischemic Heart Disease 2 (VANISH 2) trial randomised 416 patients (94% male, mean age 68 years) with prior MI and clinically significant VT within 6 months to catheter ablation vs. antiarrhythmic therapy. Catheter ablation was associated with a significant reduction in the primary composite endpoint (death or, after 14 days of treatment, appropriate implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) shock, VT storm or treated sustained VT below ICD detection threshold; 50.7% vs. 60.6%; p = 0.03) and fewer non-fatal adverse events (28.1% vs. 30.5%) although the overall mortality rate (median follow-up 4.3 years) remained high in both groups (22.2%, 25.4%) supporting the need for further evaluation of treatment strategies in this population [ 71 ]. A novel ultra-low temperature cryoablation (ULTC) catheter (vCLAS™, Adagio Medical Inc.) with the ability to titrate lesion depth and penetrate chronic scar may produce favourable results in VT ablation. Cryocure-VT was a first in-human trial evaluating endocardial ULTC ablation in 64 patients (mean age 67 years, 95.3% male, 78.1% ischaemic cardiomyopathy, mean LVEF 35 ± 10%) with recurrent, symptomatic sustained monomorphic VT despite at least one anti-arrhythmic drug. The Kaplan–Meier freedom from any sustained VT recurrence and freedom from ICD shock at 6 months was 60.3% and 81% respectively with no differences between the ischaemic and non-ischaemic cardiomyopathy [ 72 ]. Given the promising results, larger studies are in progress. CTO in patients with ischaemic cardiomyopathy is an independent risk factor for ventricular arrhythmia (VA) and the optimal timing of VT ablation in this group, in whom primary prevention ICD therapy is indicated, is uncertain [ 73 ]. In the Impact of PREVENTIVE Substrate Ablation of Coronary Chronic Total Occlusion on Implantable cardioVerTer-Defibrillator Interventions (PREVENTIVE-VT) trial, 60 patients undergoing ICD (mean age 67.5 years, 91.7% male) with ischaemic cardiomyopathy (EF ≤ 40%) and infarct-related CTO with no revascularisation option were randomised to preventative substrate ablation prior to ICD vs. ICD alone. Ablation was associated with significant reduction in the primary composite endpoint (appropriate ICD shocks or unplanned hospitalisation for symptomatic VT; 16.7% vs. 43.3%, p = 0.032), fewer inappropriate shocks (17% vs. 40%, p = 0.051), fewer VT-related hospital admissions (0% vs. 30%, p = 0.001) and fewer cardiac hospital admissions (13% vs. 53%, p = 0.002) [ 74 ]. These results support the need to highlight patients at high risk of VT in whom substrate ablation may reduce debilitating ICD shocks and hospitalisation. Novel Device Updates Leadless pacing systems may avoid the serious complications such as infection and lead fracture associated with conventional pacemakers [ 75 ]. The Dual-Chamber Leadless Pacemaker (Aveir DR i2i) Study evaluated the safety and efficacy of 300 patients who underwent implantation with the Aveir DR™ (Abbott) dual chamber leadless system. At 1 year, freedom from system-related complications was 88.6% (95% CI 84.5–91.8, p < 0.001), exceeding the performance goal of 76.5%. The composite success rate of atrial capture and sensing thresholds was 92.8% (95% CI 89.7–95.8, p < 0.001), exceeding the performance goal of 80% [ 76 ]. While early results are favourable, its current clinical use remains limited because communication between the atrial and ventricular components markedly depletes battery life (mean atrial battery life 6.4 years, ventricular battery life 11.3 years). Interest is growing in improving defibrillator lead durability and positioning to reduce adverse outcomes in patients with longer life expectancies. The Lead Evaluation for Defibrillation and Reliability (LEADR) study evaluated efficacy and safety of the OmniaSecure™ (Medtronic) lumenless, catheter-delivered lead in patients requiring ICD or cardiac resynchronisation therapy-defibrillator (CRT-D) [ 77 ]. Implantation was successful in 643 of 657 patients (97.9%) with single chamber ICD, dual chamber ICD and CRT-D implantation undertaken in 35.9%, 40.7% and 23.3%, respectively. Defibrillation was successful in 94 of 97 patients tested at implantation (exceeding the pre-specified goal of 88%) and freedom from major lead-related complications 12 months ( n = 505) was 97.1% including zero lead fractures (exceeding the pre-specified goal of 90%). Heart Failure Pharmacotherapy Semaglutide has previously been shown to improve symptoms and weight loss in patients with heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) and obesity but without diabetes [ 6 ]. The Semaglutide in Patients with Obesity-Related Heart Failure and Type 2 Diabetes (STEP-HFpEF DM) study randomised 616 patients with obesity (median body mass index [BMI] 37 kg/m 2 ), HFpEF (median NT-proBNP 493 pg/mL) and diabetes to once-weekly semaglutide 2.4 mg vs. placebo. Baseline medications included mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist in 33% and sodium–glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitor in 33%. By 52 weeks, semaglutide was associated with a greater improvement in KCCQ-clinical summary score (13.7 vs. 6.4, p < 0.001) and weight reduction (− 9.8% vs. − 3.4%; p < 0.001) [ 78 ]. Despite weight reduction, NT-proBNP also fell, suggesting benefits other than purely weight loss, meriting further study in patients without obesity. While symptom improvement is worthwhile, further studies powered to assess CV mortality and HF hospitalisations are required. Tirzepatide is a subcutaneous long-acting dual agonist (acting at the glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide receptor and glucagon-like peptide 1 [GLP-1] receptor). The Study of Tirzepatide in Participants with Heart Failure with Preserved Ejection Fraction and Obesity (SUMMIT) randomised 731 patients with HF, LVEF of at least 50% and BMI of at least 30 kg/m 2 to 15 mg weekly of tirzepatide vs. placebo. At 52 weeks, tirzepatide was associated with significant symptom improvement (mean between-group KCCQ-CSS difference + 6.9; p < 0.001) and a reduction in the composite of CV death and HF hospitalisation (HR 0.62; p < 0.026) although low absolute numbers of events limit interpretation and further larger trials are required [ 79 ]. Nonsteroidal mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists such as finerenone are another medication class hoped to advance the currently limited pharmacological management of HFpEF. The Finerenone in Heart Failure with Mildly Reduced or Preserved Ejection Fraction (FINEARTS-HF) trial randomized 6001 patients with HF and an ejection fraction of ≥ 40% to finerenone vs. placebo. Use of finerenone was associated with a 16% reduction in the primary outcome (worsening HF events or CV mortality) at a median of 32 months (rate ratio, 0.84; 95% CI 0.74–0.95; p = 0.007) driven primarily by heart failure events, defined as a first or recurrent unplanned hospitalisation or urgent visit for HF (rate ratio, 0.82; 95% CI 0.71–0.94; p = 0.006) with no significant difference in CV mortality (HR 0.93; 95% CI 0.78–1.11) [ 80 ]. Interestingly, the effect of finerenone appeared independent of SGLT2i and consistent across the ejection fraction range measured. A new paradigm of three-pillar therapy for heart failure with preserved ejection may be emerging, consisting of SGLT 2 inhibitors, GLP-1 agonists and nonsteroidal mineralocorticoid antagonists, albeit noting that definite reduction of CV mortality in this population remains elusive. Building on the previously discussed DAPA-MI [ 6 ], the Empagliflozin after Acute Myocardial Infarction (EMPACT-MI) trial randomised 6522 patients with acute MI and increased risk of HF (new LVEF ≤ 45% or clinical congestion) to empagliflozin vs. placebo [ 81 ]. At a median of 17.9 months, empagliflozin only showed a numerical but no significant reduction in the primary composite endpoint of death or HF hospitalisation (8.2% vs. 9.1%; p = 0.21) but there was significant 23% reduction in first HF hospitalisation (3.6% vs. 4.7%; HR 0.77; 95% CI 0.60–0.98). In keeping with DAPA-MI, this suggests that SGLT2is do not reduce mortality post MI, but their use is still worthwhile to reduce HF readmission. The Electronic Nicotine Product Use is Associated with Incident Heart Failure—The All of Us Research Program study [ 82 ] prospectively assessed 175,667 participants’ survey data and Electronic Health Records (EHR) by Cox proportional hazards models adjusted for demographic and socioeconomic factors, diabetes mellitus, hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, BMI and concomitant substance use (cigarette, cigar, hookah, smokeless cigarette and alcohol use). At a median of 45 months follow-up, anytime electronic cigarette use vs. no use was associated with a 19% increase in incident HF failure (adjusted HR 1.19 (95% CI 1.06–1.35) adding to the emerging health concerns associated with electronic cigarettes. Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy The ESC 2023 Cardiomyopathy Guidelines recommended consideration of mavacamten, a cardiac myosin inhibitor, in addition to a beta-blocker (or verapamil/diltiazem if contraindicated) for patients with symptomatic obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) [ 83 ]. The Safety, Efficacy, and Quantitative Understanding of Obstruction Impact of Aficamten in Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (SEQUOIA-HCM) trial randomized 282 patients with symptomatic obstructive HCM to aficamten (another cardiac myosin inhibitor) vs. placebo. At 24 weeks, aficamten was associated with significant improvement in the primary endpoint of peak VO2 (mean 1.8 vs. 0.0 ml/kg/min improvement from baseline; p < 0.0001), improved KCCQ-CSS (12 vs. 5; p < 0.001), and decreased LV outflow tract (LVOT) gradient (− 47.6 vs. 1.8 mmHg; p < 0.001), without an excess of serious adverse events (5.6% vs. 9.3%) [ 84 ]. A head-to-head comparison of aficamten vs. mavacatem would now be of interest. In contrast, no approved medical therapies currently exist for patients with non-obstructive HCM. Ninerafaxstat is a novel cardiac mitotrope which enhances cardiac energetics. The Study to Evaluate the Safety, Tolerability, and Efficacy of IMB-1018972 in Patients With Non-obstructive Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (IMPROVE HCM) trial randomised 67 patients with HCM and LVOT gradient < 30 mmHg, LVEF > 50% and peak oxygen consumption < 80% of predicted to ninerafaxstat vs. placebo [ 85 ]. At 12 weeks, ninerafaxstat was associated with improved ventilatory efficiency (VE/VCO2 slope least-squares (LS) mean difference of − 2.1 (95% CI − 3.6 to − 0.6; p = 0.006), and in post hoc analysis, improved KCCQ-CSS in the subgroup with baseline KCCQ-CSS ≤ 80 ( n = 35; LS mean 9.4; 95% CI 0.3–18.5; p = 0.04). The phase II findings are encouraging and support ongoing phase III study. Amyloidosis Transthyretin amyloidosis with cardiomyopathy (ATTR-CM) is a progressive, fatal disease with scant treatment options. Vutrisiran is a small interfering RNA agent which inhibits the production of hepatic transthyretin. The Study to Evaluate Vutrisiran in Patients With Transthyretin Amyloidosis With Cardiomyopathy (HELIOS-B) randomised 655 patients with ATTR-CM to vutrisiran vs. placebo ( n = 329) for up to 36 months. Vutrisiran was associated with a 28% reduction in death or recurrent CV events (HR 0.72; 95% CI 0.56–0.93; p = 0.01), and 35% reduction in death up to 42 months (HR 0.65; 95% CI 0.46–0.90; p = 0.01), with reduced decline in 6-min walk test (6MWT) and distance and KCCQ-OS score (LS mean differences 26.5 m; 95% CI 13.4–39.6; p < 0.001 and 5.8 points; 95% CI 2.4–9.2; p < 0.001, respectively) [ 86 ]. Similarly positive results were observed in the subgroup of patients not receiving tafamidis at baseline. Adverse event rates were similar to placebo. These findings suggest vutrisiran may be a highly promising new treatment for ATTR-CM. It has previously been suggested that an interatrial shunt may provide an autoregulatory mechanism to decrease left atrial pressure and improve heart failure outcomes. The REducing Lung congestIon Symptoms Using the v-wavE Shunt in adVancEd Heart Failure (RELIEVE HF) study randomised 508 patients with heart failure, stratified to LVEF ≤ 40% ( n = 206) and LVEF > 40% ( n = 302), to transcatheter implantation of an interatrial shunt vs. a sham procedure. At 2 years, use of interatrial shunt was not found to improve the primary composite endpoint of death, cardiac transplantation, LV assist device implantation, HF hospitalisation, outpatient worsening HF events or KCCQ-OS (win ratio, 0.86 [95% CI 0.61–1.22]; p = 0.20) [ 87 ]. A possible benefit for HFrEF and harm for HFpEF was suggested in subgroup analysis, but the trial was underpowered to evaluate this fully. Optimising Clinical Practice Given the increasing cost of inpatient HF care, the Evaluating the Effectiveness of Remote Patient Monitoring (Satelia® Cardio) for Patients With Heart Failure (TELESAT HF) study compared use of Satelia Cardio Remote Patient Monitoring [RPM] (Satelia, Bordeaux, France) ( n = 5357) incorporating symptom/weight monitoring and education vs. a propensity-matched usual care HF group ( n = 13,525) [ 88 ]. RPM was associated with a 36% lower risk of death (HR 0.64, 95% CI 0.58–0.68, p < 0.0001), an effect which appeared consistent across subgroups. Whilst encouraging, future RCTs are required to confirm this observational finding. The implementation of guideline-directed medical therapy remains suboptimal. ESC guidelines recommend patients with HFrEF rapidly reach maximally tolerated doses of four-pillar therapy. The first report from the Titration of Treatment in Heart Failure (TITRATE-HF) prospective registry of consecutive patients with HF and LVEF < 50% reported that while 44% of patients with chronic and worsening HFrEF were prescribed quadruple therapy, only 1% achieved target dose for all drugs [ 89 ]. Patients treated in a dedicated HF clinic vs. general cardiology clinic were more likely to be prescribed quadruple therapy compared to a general cardiology clinic (47% vs. 37%). The rationale for non-use was often not documented. Similar findings from the USA were noted in analysis of the Get With The Guidelines Heart Failure registry , from which 33,036 patients had been hospitalised with new HFrEF from 2016 to 2023. Overall, 82% of patients were deemed eligible for quadruple therapy and 93% eligible for triple therapy. Sub-analysis of the 14,303 patients hospitalised from 2021 to 2023 showed beta-blockers were the most commonly prescribed discharge medication (92.2%), followed by ACEi/ARB/ARNI (75.8%), MRA (41.0%), then SGLT2i (23.5%); and of those eligible for quadruple therapy, only 15.3% received quadruple therapy and 41.5% triple therapy [ 90 ]. It was extrapolated that full implementation of quadruple therapy at discharge might lead to over 10% and 24% absolute reductions in 12 month mortality vs. double therapy or no therapy, respectively (Fig. 2 ). Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Central illustration from Greene et al. [ 90 ] demonstrating US HF registry data of patients hospitalised with new HFrEF from 2016 to 2023. a Proportion of patients eligible for quadruple therapy. b HF medication prescribed on discharge. c Estimated reduction in all-cause mortality from addition of GDMT in eligible patients. Image reprinted under Creative Commons CC-BY-NC open access agreement, with permission from Oxford University Press. ACEi angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, ARB angiotensin receptor blocker, ARNI angiotensin receptor/neprilysin inhibitor, BB beta-blocker, GDMT guideline-directed medical therapy, MRA mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, RASi renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system inhibitors, SGLT2i sodium–glucose cotransporter 2 In the Timely Evaluation and Management of Advanced Heart Failure (TEAM-HF) trial, outcomes of 114 patients with HF and LVEF < 50% who underwent rapid titration via a dedicated HF clinic were compared with a matched control group receiving usual care. Those attending the dedicated clinic over a median 15.8 weeks increased their four-pillar heart failure therapy from 21% to 88% ( p < 0.001), achieved higher drug doses vs. usual care, reduced NYHA II/III HF symptoms from 94% to 75% ( p < 0.001) and reduced median BNP from 587 to 534 ng/L ( p = 0.03) [ 91 ]. Prevention Dyslipidaemia The importance of early low-density lipoprotein (LDL)-cholesterol lowering is well recognised. A Randomized Study to Evaluate the Effect of an “Inclisiran First” Implementation Strategy Compared to Usual Care in Patients With Atherosclerotic Cardiovascular Disease and Elevated LDL-C Despite Receiving Maximally Tolerated Statin Therapy (VICTORION-INITIATE) randomised 450 patients with atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) and LDL-C > 1.8 mmol/l (mean 2.5 mmol/l) or non-high density lipoprotein (HDL)-C > 2.6 mmol/l to immediate initiation of the siRNA therapy inclisiran on top of usual care vs. usual care alone. At 330 days, use of inclisiran was associated with significantly greater reduction in LDL-C (60% vs. 7%; p < 0.001) and more patients achieved ≥ 50% reduction in LDL-C (69.8% vs. 5.3%; p < 0.001) [ 92 ]. This highlights the early efficacy achievable with inclisiran although we await results from the phase III outcomes trial to help analyse cost-effectiveness. The benefit of early and sustained lipid lowering was also confirmed by new data from the SWEDEHEART registry study which reported outcomes over a median of 5.4 years for 56,262 patients post MI [ 93 ]. Achieving non-HDL-C < 2.2 mmol/l early (at 2 months) and late (at 1 year) had the best outcome with 20% reduction in MACE (death, MI or ischaemic stroke) (HR 0.80, 95% CI 0.74–0.86) vs. only achieving non-HDL-C < 2.2 mmol/l at 2 months or 1 year but not both (HR 0.86, 95% CI 0.79–0.93) vs. never achieving non-HDL-C < 2.2 mmol/l (Fig. 3 ). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Central illustration from Schubert et al. [ 93 ] demonstrating the superiority of early and sustained reduction in non-HDL vs. other strategies (HR 0.80, 95% CI 0.74–0.86). Image reprinted under Creative Commons CC-BY-NC open access agreement, with permission from Elseveir. CI confidence interval, HDL-C high-density lipoprotein, HR hazard ratio, MACE major adverse cardiovascular events, MI myocardial infarction Achieving intensive lipid lowering may be facilitated by proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9 (PCSK9) inhibition. The LIBerating High-Risk (LIBerate-HR) study [ 94 ] randomised patients (mean age 65 years, 45% female, at high/very high risk of CV disease, already on statin, mean LDL-C 116 mg/dL) to monthly subcutaneous administration of lerodalcibep 300 mg, a novel injectable small binding protein PCSK9 inhibitor, ( n = 615) vs. placebo ( n = 307). At 52 weeks (88% follow-up), lerodalcibep was associated with a 56.2% placebo-adjusted reduction in LDL-C ( p < 0.001) and 94% achieved ≥ 50% LDL-C reduction ( p < 0.001) without an excess of adverse events. Another potential lipid target is apolipoprotein C3 (APOC3)-mediated inhibition of lipoprotein lipase, which downregulates metabolism and clearance of triglyceride (TG)-rich lipoproteins. Olezarsen is a novel antisense oligonucleotide APOC messenger RNA. Bergmark et al. conducted a phase 2b RCT enrolling 154 patients (median age 62 years, 42% female, 97% on lipid-lowering therapy) with moderate TG elevation (1.7–5.6 mmol/l) plus high CV risk, or severe TG elevation alone (≥ 5.6 mmol/l), who were randomised to monthly olezarsen 50 mg ( n = 58) vs. 80 mg ( n = 57) vs. placebo ( n = 39). At 6 months, olezarsen was associated with a significant TG reduction from baseline (mean 49.3% with 50 mg; mean 53.1% with 80 mg; p < 0.001) vs. 7.8% with placebo [ 95 ]. Plozasiran is a hepatocyte-targeted APOC3 small interfering RNA. In the Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled Phase 2b Study to Evaluate the Efficacy and Safety of ARO-APOC3 in Adults With Mixed Dyslipidemia (MUIR) study, 353 patients with TG elevation (1.7–5.6 mmol/l) were randomised to plozasiran 10 mg, or 25 mg, or 50 mg (day 1 and week 12), plozasiran 50 mg (day 1 and week 24) or placebo (day 1 and week 12). At 24 weeks, significant reductions from baseline in the fasting TG were observed with all plozasiran regimens vs. placebo (mean reduction ranging from − 44.2 to − 62.4%; p < 0.001) [ 96 ]. Of note, worsening glycaemic control was observed in 1/5 of patients with the 50 mg dose. Outcomes will be assessed further in an ongoing phase III trial. Lipoprotein (Lp) ( a ) is now recognised as a causal risk factor for CV disease. Muvalaplin is an oral small molecule that inhibits assembly of Lp( a ) by disrupting bonding of apo(a) to apoB. The Study of LY3473329 in Adult Participants With Elevated Lipoprotein(a) at High Risk for Cardiovascular Events (KRAKEN), [ 97 ] randomised 233 patients (mean age 64 years, 32% female) aged ≥ 40 years at high CV risk and elevated Lp( a ) ≥ 175 nmol/L to muvalaplin 10 mg, 60 mg, 240 mg or placebo. At a median follow-up of 12 weeks, muvalaplin was associated with significant placebo-adjusted Lp( a ) reduction (10 mg = 47.6%, 60 mg = 81.7%, 240 mg = 85.8%) using an intact Lp( a ) assay methodology since muvalaplin binding causes overestimation of Lp( a ) with conventional assays [ 98 ]. Zerlasiran is a subcutaneous siRNA biologic agent which reduces Lp( a ) production. The Assessment of Lipoprotein(a) Lowering in Patients with Atherosclerotic Cardiovascular Disease (ALPACAR-360) trial randomised 178 participants (mean age 64, 26% female) to zerlasiran 450 mg every 24 weeks for two doses, 300 mg every 16 weeks for three doses, 300 mg every 24 weeks for two doses, or placebo. At 36 weeks, the three zerlasiran treatment regimens were associated with significant time-averaged reductions in Lp( a ) of 85.6%, 82.8% and 81.3%, respectively [ 98 ]. Now that effective therapies are available to reduce serum Lp( a ), results from large CV outcomes trials—Lp( a )HORIZON using pelacarsen and OCEAN(a)-Outcomes using olpasiran—are eagerly awaited in 2025–2026 [ 99 – 101 ]. A summary of completed and ongoing trials targeting Lp( a ) is provided in Table 1 . Table 1. Summary of recently completed and ongoing clinical studies evaluating Lp( a ) pharmacotherapy [ 98 , 99 ] Current and ongoing studies evaluating Lp( a ) pharmacotherapy Study Design n Drug Mechanism Primary endpoint Completion KRAKEN Phase II, RCT 233 Muvalaplin (Oral) Inhibits Lp( a ) assembly % change in Lp( a ) from baseline to 12 weeks Mar 2024 ALPACAR-360 Phase II, RCT 178 Zerlasiran (SC) Small interfering RNA % change in Lp( a ) from baseline to 36 weeks Jul 2024 OCEAN(a)-Outcomes Phase III, RCT tbc Olpasiran (SC) Small interfering RNA Time to CHD death, myocardial infarction, or urgent coronary revascularisation Estimated 2026 Lp( a ) HORIZON Phase III, RCT tbc Pelacarsen (SC) Inhibits hepatic apo(a) synthesis Time to MACE delineated by Lp( a ) ≥ 70 and ≥ 90 mg/dL Estimated 2026 ACCLAIM-Lp( a ) Phase III, RCT tbc Lepodisiran (SC) Small interfering RNA Time to MACE Estimated 2029 Open in a new tab CHD coronary heart disease, Lp(a) lipoprotein ( a ), MACE major adverse cardiovascular event, RCT randomised controlled trial, RNA ribonucleic acid, SC subcutaneous, tbc to be confirmed Despite advances in new lipid-lowering therapies, ensuring eligible patients receive even basic treatments is likely to have the largest population benefit. In the Technology-Assisted Cholesterol Trial in Consumers (TACTiC) study, 12,624 participants entered demographic and medical information into a web app, of whom 1196 (median age 63 years, 60.4% male, mean LDL-C 3.6 mmol/l) received a web app outcome of ‘ok to use’ or ‘ask a doctor’ regarding eligibility for rosuvastatin 5 mg. The web app outcome showed 90.7% concordance with later blinded clinician assessment. At 6 months, adherence was 95% based on pill counts, mean LDL-C reduction was 35.5% and 7.1% experiencing adverse effects leading to discontinuation [ 102 ]. Artificial Intelligence and Imaging Innovative technology may also improve efficiency in diagnostic imaging. The PROspective randomised controlled Trial Evaluating the Use of artificial intelligence in Stress echocardiography (PROTEUS) randomised 2341 patients across 20 UK hospitals with suspected coronary artery disease undergoing stress echocardiography to AI-augmented decision making vs. usual care [ 103 ]. Overall, use of AI-augmentation did not meet prespecified non-inferiority criteria. Of those referred for angiography, 34/49 (69%) AI referrals vs. 27/36 (75%) usual care referrals were correct; of those not sent for an angiogram who subsequently experienced an event at 6 months, 19 were in the AI group vs. 22 in the control group. However, subgroup analyses suggested AI may benefit clinical decision-making for less experienced clinicians and in more complex patients. Hypertension Management Despite lifestyle modifications and drug therapies, hypertension continues to be a significant contributing factor in the worldwide incidence of coronary artery disease, stroke, and kidney failure. Zilebesiran is a new siRNA agent which blocks the hepatic production of angiotensin. At ACC, Desai presented data from their phase II trial [ 104 ] enrolling 672 patients (mean age 53 years, 43% female) with uncontrolled hypertension despite baseline treatment with either indapamide 2.5 mg or amlodipine 5 mg or olmesartan 40 mg daily who were randomised to a single subcutaneous injection of zilebesiran 600 mg vs. placebo. At 3 months, Zilebesiran was associated with significant reduction of mean 24-h systolic blood pressure (SBP) on top of each baseline therapy (indapamide group − 12.1 mmHg, p < 0.001; amlodipine group − 9.7 mmHg; p < 0.001; olmesartan group − 4.0 mmHg, p = 0.036). Optimal hypertension treatment targets continued to be debated. The Blood Pressure Control Target in Diabetes (BPROAD) trial [ 105 ] randomised 12,821 patients (mean age 64 years, 45.3% female) with type 2 diabetes mellitus to intensive (target SBP ≤ 120 mmHg) vs. standard treatment (target SBP ≤ 140 mmHg). Intensive treatment was associated with a lower mean SBP at 1 year (121.6 vs. 133.2 mmHg) and a 21% reduction in the primary composite outcome (stroke, MI, heart failure, or CV death) at a median of 4.2 years (HR 0.79; 95% CI 0.69–0.90; p < 0.001). Findings are consistent with the Systolic Blood Pressure Intervention Trial (SPRINT), the Effects of Intensive Systolic Blood Pressure Lowering Treatment in Reducing Risk of Vascular Events (ESPRIT) trial and in keeping with BP target reductions in 2024 ESC guidelines. Debate continues over the safety and efficacy of taking antihypertensive medication at bedtime instead of the morning. The Bedtime versus morning use of antihypertensives for cardiovascular risk reduction (BedMed) trial enrolled 3357 patients with hypertension managed in primary care (median age 67 years), and the BedMed-Frail trial enrolled 776 nursing home residents [ 106 ]. In both trials patients were randomized to bedtime vs. morning BP medication. At follow-up bedtime dosing was not associated with any reduction in the primary outcome of death, ACS hospitalisation, stroke, or heart failure in either BedMed (HR 0.96; 95% CI 0.77–1.19 at a median of 4.6 years) or BedMed-Frail (HR 0.88; 95% CI 0.71–1.11 at 415 days). In keeping with prior studies these trials support taking BP medication when most convenient for patients, which may aid adherence. Use of single-pill combinations (SPCs) has been restricted because of uncertainties regarding preferred drug components and doses. Following a 4-week run in, Rodgers et al. randomised 1385 patients with hypertension (mean clinic BP 142/85 mmHg on 1.6 antihypertensive medications) to GMRx2 half dose (telmisartan 20 mg, amlodipine 2.5 mg, indapamide 1.25 mg), telmisartan 20 mg with amlodipine 2.5 mg, telmisartan 20 mg with indapamide 1.25 mg, or amlodipine 2.5 mg with indapamide 1.25 mg. Doses were doubled in all groups at 6 weeks if no contraindication. At week 12, GMRx2 was associated with more frequent achievement of clinic BP < 140/90 mmHg, and mean home SBP with GMRx2 (126 mmHg) was superior to the other dual drug combinations (− 2.5 mmHg, p < 0.0001 vs. telmisartan-indapamide, − 5.4 mmHg, p < 0.0001 vs. telmisartan-amlodipine and − 4.4 mmHg, p < 0.0001 vs. amlodipine-indapamide) [ 107 ]. In summary the low-dose triple combination was well tolerated and provided BP superior control. As there was no significant difference in treatment withdrawals due to adverse events and by reducing pill burden, the SPC may improve adherence. Further evaluating the role of combination therapy, following an 8-week run-in of three separate tablet therapies with perindopril, indapamide and amlodipine (10/2.5/5 mg or 10/2.5/10 mg), the QUADruplet therapy for Resistant hypertension with Oral medication (QUADRO) trial randomised 183 patients to a quadruple SPC (containing bisoprolol 5 mg plus the prior three drugs) vs. the ongoing three separate tablets. After 8 weeks, quadruple SPC was associated improved reduction in the primary endpoint of mean office SBP (− 8.04 mmHg; p < 0.0001), reduction in mean 24-h SBP (− 7.53 mmHg; p < 0.0001) and achievement of office BP < 140/90 mmHg (66.3% vs. 42.7%, p = 0.001), home BP < 135/85 mmHg (60.7% vs. 25.4%, p < 0.0001) and ambulatory BP < 130/80 mmHg (51.2% vs. 20.7%, p < 0.0001) with no significant differences in adverse events observed [ 108 ]. QUADRO suggests a quadruple SPC may improve control in resistant hypertension and may improve adherence. The role and optimal technique of renal denervation in patients with resistant hypertension remains to be fully established with efficacy and safety of radio-frequency and ultrasound being established in sham-controlled trials [ 11 ]. The TARGET BP I investigators evaluated the use of alcohol delivered via the Peregrine® micro needle infusion system into the renal perivascular space. In total 301 patients with uncontrolled hypertension (office systolic BP (SBP) ≥ 150 and ≤ 180 mmHg, office diastolic BP ≥ 90 mmHg, mean 24-h ambulatory SBP ≥ 135 to ≤ 170 mmHg, despite 2–5 antihypertensive drugs) were randomised to renal alcohol denervation vs. sham control. Use of renal denervation was associated with a modest reduction in the primary endpoint of 24-h ambulatory SBP at 3 months (− 10.0 vs. − 6.8 mmHg; p = 0.0487) but only a numerical, non-significant reduction in office SBP (− 12.7 vs. − 9.7 mmHg; p = 0.173) and no significant reduction in ambulatory or office diastolic BP [ 109 ]. While the trial was technically positive, the rather modest treatment benefit in TARGET BP I and lack, to date, of long-term CV outcome data suggest that ongoing studies are needed to define optimal procedural techniques and the patient groups most likely to benefit. Conclusion This paper has summarised a wide array of key trials in clinical cardiology that were presented and/or published during 2024. This year, we have seen the incorporation of intravascular imaging as a class IA recommendation for PCI in complex coronary lesions. Renewed interest has also been noted in the use of DCBs; however, positive data appears to be restricted to small vessel coronary artery disease. In acute coronary syndromes, we have seen a consolidation of evidence for abbreviated DAPT with evidence supporting its use in ACS, building on previously published data. Notable advances in structural intervention were seen with important data from NOTION-3 providing important evidence for guiding necessity of PCI in TAVI along with interesting data from TRISCEND II and TRILLUMINATE, which may provide impetus for an expanded future role for transcatheter intervention in patients with symptomatic severe TR. Additionally, in electrophysiology and devices, several studies have presented interesting data regarding PFA, a novel therapy for catheter ablation of AF, which in the future may supersede traditional ablation techniques. A considerable number of important trials have been published in the field of HF and CMs which may change clinical practice. In particular, the evaluation of novel pharmacotherapies such as the dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist tirzepatide, the nonsteroidal mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist finerenone and the cardiac myosin inhibitor aficamen. Finally, in the field of prevention we have seen an expansion in novel therapeutics for the treatment of dyslipidaemias in addition to several promising potential drug targets from early phase clinical trials which in the future may yield novel avenues for the treatment of ASCVD. Acknowledgments Authorship All named authors meet the International Committee of Medical Journal Editors (ICMJE) criteria for authorship for this manuscript, take responsibility for the integrity of the work as a whole, and have given final approval for the version to be published. Author Contributions Patrick Savage—Lead author, collated clinical trials for review and divided sections across authors. Wrote structural, ACS and antiplatelet sections as well as abstract, introduction and compilation of all sections into one document. Implemented final edits from supervising consultant and reviewers. Michael Campbell—Wrote intervention section. Daniel McElhatton—Heart failure and prevention section; Meadhbh Hogg— EP/Devices section/prevention; Ian Menown—Supervising consultant. Guided style and format of review paper. Comprehensive review and editing and revision of the full draft. All authors have approved the final submission. The lead author has reviewed the final edit of this manuscript. Funding No funding or sponsorship was received for this study or publication of this article. Declarations Conflict of Interest Ian Menown has received grants to institution, honoraria and/or conference sponsorship from Biosensors and Novartis. Ian Menown is an editorial board member of Advances in Therapy . Ian Menown was not involved in the selection of peer reviewers for the manuscript nor any of the subsequent editorial decisions. Patrick Savage, Michael Campbell, Daniel McElhatton and Meadhbh Hogg have nothing to disclose. Ethical Approval This article is based on previously conducted studies and does not involve any new studies of human or animal subjects performed by any of the authors. References 1. Vrints C,