INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(126,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12327526','India cardiology training curriculum- a critical reappraisal: Is it the time to rethink?','Kapoor A, Sethi R, Yadav R.','2025','rigors amalgamate restructured courage rejecting msr relook visionary bidhan spearheaded vellore kem aiims doyens khalliullah tandon sukumar cherian datey vakil ambit erstwhile nbe nbe drnb nbe repealed nbe neet cum superspecialty aiims pgimer chandigarh jipmer puducherry nimhans bengaluru sctimst thiruvananthapuram ambit neet msr nbe drnb msr pgmeb msr pgmeb notifies msr nbems complying nbems drnb fnb earmarked relook restructure yesterday underemphasized mandatorily cath rote nstead repetitively superannuated nbe mous memorandums cmes cmes familiarizing weightage zonal superannuated icmr aortoarteritis viva voce inexplicably eeec venture examinee electrograms replicable apsc eecc apsc apsc apsc eecc ahftc revamp rote acumen padmavati 3e4 ihj deb ihj accr natboard online_user 20notices 53f6qc accr natboard online_user frontpage accr natboard online_user frontpage mcirest getdocument latestnews msr accr natboard online_user misc information_bulletin_chapter ozair ehac140 duijn welink bok ehaa641 ramnanan pound s109037 flipping ochsner chuan foe musunuru haspel circgenetics gosai evidencing latestin simulationbased schimmel sweis cathet voelker tonissen beginners joic damp bok drazen aboutama interactive_0 michalis ehae453 kcsntt1c apsc e24','Abstract The challenges and rigors of the modern-day health care systems demand a critical reappraisal of our training paradigms in cardiology. Today, modern day DM and DNB Cardiology training needs to seamlessly amalgamate traditional teaching methodologies with the rapidly evolving technology based educational tools now available to us for personalized and adaptive learning. The contemporary cardiology curricula need to incorporate ALL components of clinical competency including cognitive, psychomotor and affective skills to enable the next generation of cardiologists to provide truly holistic care to their patients. In addition, a greater focus on impactful cardiology research with an intent to publish it while in training, is likely to encourage at least some of the young trainees to pursue careers in academia. Most importantly, the exit examination patterns need to be restructured. We need to decide whether we need cardiologists who simply follow textbooks and are trained in procedures —or we need those who have the ability to themselves write the next chapters in cardiology, have the precision of thought, the depth of empathy, and the courage to question. Keywords: DM cardiology, Training, Curriculum, Reappraisal A paradigm shift in cardiology training in India is a challenge that does not mean rejecting the past—it means refining it, questioning it, and reshaping it for a better tomorrow. The paper discusses the following pertinent issues. (A) Genesis of Cardiology training in India (B) Pattern of DM and DNB Cardiology Courses in India (C) Minimum standard of requirements (MSR) (D) What are the issues that merit a relook with regard to Cardiology training in India (A) Genesis of Cardiology training in India : The history of modern cardiology in India dates back to 4th April, 1948, when the visionary Dr. Bidhan Chandra Roy motivated a group of Indian physicians to officially form the Cardiological Society of India (CSI). 1 , 2 The newly formed society, that pre-dated even the inception of American College of Cardiology by one year, spearheaded the campaign against various cardiovascular disease (CVD) including rheumatic heart disease, hypertension and coronary artery disease, by organizing meetings and conferences and creating public awareness across the country. Taking note of these initiatives, the Government of India and the state governments too, focused attention on the burden of CVD and helped set up Cardiology departments in various parts of India. Some of the earliest such departments to come into existence in early 1950''s, were at Vellore, Tamil Nadu, KEM Mumbai and the All-India Institute of Medical Sciences, Delhi. The Governing Body of AIIMS decided to establish one of the first DM Cardiology programs in 1957 and this was followed by the first-ever cardiac catheterisation laboratory for clinical studies in early 1960''s. During this time, doyens of Cardiology like Dr ML Bhatia, Dr M Khalliullah, Dr R Tandon in the north, Dr I P Sukumar and Dr K M Cherian in the south, Dr. K K Datey and Dr. R J Vakil in the West and Dr. R N Chatterjee and Dr. GC Gupta in the East inspired and mentored generations of Cardiologists as part of their DM Cardiology training programs to carry forward the mantle of Indian cardiology. 2 (B) Established Pattern of DM and DNB Cardiology Courses in India: The DM Cardiology programs (like all other DM courses) initially were for two years but later transitioned to three years and are currently under the ambit of the National Medical Commission ( NMC ) that came into force on 25.9.2020 after the dissolution of the erstwhile Medical Council of India. In 1975, the Government of India established the National Board of Examinations (NBE) with the objective of improving the quality of the Medical Education by establishing high and uniform standards of postgraduate examinations in modern medicine on an All-India Basis and utilizing existing healthcare infrastructure for capacity building. The NBE introduced the Diplomate in National Board (DNB) and the super specialty (Doctorate of National Board-DrNB) 3- year courses for post MD/MS/DNB students wherein candidates could be admitted to affiliated institutions under the guidance of recognized post graduate teachers. The qualifications awarded by NBE in various Broad Specialties, Super specialties and Sub-Specialties are approved by the Government of India and included in the First Schedule of IMC (repealed) Act 1956/NMC Act 2019. 3 , 4 The NBE provides a common national standard for examinations in the form of the NEET-SS examination (the National Eligibility cum Entrance Test for Super Speciality courses) that is a single window entrance examination for admission to DM/MCh and (Post MD/MS/DNB) DNB Superspecialty Courses nationwide. Some institutes like AIIMS, New Delhi, PGIMER, Chandigarh, JIPMER, Puducherry, NIMHANS, Bengaluru and SCTIMST, Thiruvananthapuram conduct their own entrance examinations and are not under the ambit of NEET-SS. (C) Minimum standard of requirements (MSR) : At present, India offers 706 accredited positions for cardiology training , 528 by the NMC (DM Cardiology) and 202 by the NBE (DrNB Cardiology). 5 , 6 Of the 528 DM Cardiology seats , offered by 133 colleges , 81 belong to the government sector (total DM seats offered 374), while 52 are in corporate institutes (DM seats 154). Since India has a large pool of cardiology trainees, it is very important to understand if the training standards are uniform and quality-controlled? The currently defined Minimum standard of requirements (MSR) for accreditation is as follows: 1. Medical colleges/institutes are expected to follow the guidelines prescribed by the Post-graduate Medical Education Board (PGMEB) in the MSR document for post-graduate medical education. 2. The PGMEB notifies about the MSR from time to time for physical infrastructure of the hospital and medical college/institute, teaching staff and other staff, clinical material (prescribed number of patients as per curriculum requirement, laboratory and radiological investigations) for all-round training of the post-graduate students. 7 3. An annual review of accreditation is usually undertaken by NBEMS to ensure that the hospital is complying with the minimum NBEMS accreditation criteria. The accredited DNB/DrNB/FNB seat of a department may be excluded from the counselling seat matrix if the hospital fails to successfully complete the annual review of the concerned specialty. 8 Although these processes are well outlined, it is imperative to ensure appropriate monitoring and oversight of standards, that institutes need to meet for continued accreditation . The Core Cardiology Training guidelines in the West are regularly updated and have clearly earmarked expectations from trainees at different levels of competence, for both general cardiology and subsequent sub-specialty training. 9 (D) What are the issues that merit a relook with regard to Cardiology training in India: 1 Curriculum 2 Training schedules and Teaching methods 3 Need to set individual departmental protocols and resident orientations 4 Research and competency skill development 5 Restructure the exit exam system 6 Sub-speciality training (1) Curriculum: If we teach today''s students as we taught yesterday''s, we rob them of an enriching tomorrow. • The DM course curricula have to be tailor made to specific disease subsets that are contextual and relevant to regional, national and global health issues. Importantly, the course curriculum needs regular revisions to remain relevant to evolving medical advancements and patient needs. • The curricula should include preventive cardiology, AI in diagnostics, interventional cardiology, precision medicine, digital health applications, principles of ethics and research, molecular biology and applications, statistical tools, experimental design and research methodology, biomedical physics etc. • The Cardiology curriculum should clearly define the final program outcomes wherein the DM trainee student should acquire the following competencies under three domains : o Cognitive domain (Knowledge domain) o Psychomotor domain (technical skills) o Affective domain (Attitudes: Communication and Professionalism) • Unfortunately, most of our current curricula focus on theoretical knowledge (cognitive domain) and technical skills (psychomotor domain). However, the affective domain , which includes communication skills, empathy, ethical practice, and patient-centred care, is often underemphasized. Discussions on medical ethics and professionalism should be a part of training by having structured courses on doctor-patient communication, shared decision-making and breaking bad news. • The ESC core curriculum for Cardiology mandatorily requires each trainee to acquire clinical competency based on (i) knowledge, (ii) skills, and (iii) attitudes (including integrity, reliability, and humility because although knowledge is necessary, it needs to supplemented by appropriate attitudinal skills. 10 , 11 • In the final assessment & evaluation of our trainees, affective domain parameters should also be included with a 360-degree workplace-based assessment wherein feedback can be sought from peers, teachers, nurses, administrative staff and even patients to gain insights regarding communication skills, team working, and reliability. 11 • By balancing the triad of cognitive, psychomotor, and affective domains , DM/DNB Cardiology programs can train more well-rounded and empathetic specialists , thereby improving patient outcomes. (2) Training schedules and student-centric teaching methods : For the last many years, the traditional DM/DNB Cardiology training schedules in India have followed pre-defined OPD, Cath Lab, and Non-Invasive Lab postings varying from 6 to 12 months over a 3- year period apart from ward, ICU, Cardiac surgery and other rotations. Conventional Cardiology teaching in India has heavily relied on lecture-based learning, case presentations, and bedside teaching. While these methods are indeed valuable, they often lack active student engagement, problem-solving focus and participatory learning. The current system prioritizes rote learning and memorization over critical thinking and independent decision-making , making it perhaps somewhat outdated in today''s rapidly evolving medical landscape. • Moreover, since different students may have varying learning and comprehension abilities, simple didactic teaching may lead to non-uniform learning outcomes. There is an urgent need of greater flexibility and influx of newer training methodologies to keep pace with the advancements in technology and patient care approaches. Student centric methods like experiential, participatory and interactive teaching and multidisciplinary discussions offer an alternative to the traditional “one-size-fits-all” educational model. • Flipped Classroom: In the Flipped Classroom Model, i nstead of didactic lectures by teachers, the students can study directly through multimodality instructional material including videos, online courses, and case studies in advance before discussing in the classroom and work collaboratively on assigned tasks leading to improved performances. 12 , 13 , 14 , 15 • Virtual reality (VR) and Simulation-Based Learning for Echo, Angiography & Interventions allow students to reduce learning curves and practice complex diagnostic and intervention procedures in a controlled setting. Skills can be refined and procedural errors corrected, thus potentially reducing the risk to real patients by allowing prior simulator-based practise. 16 , 17 , 18 , 19 , 20 Various clinical and emergency scenarios can be replicated by high-fidelity simulations ensuring uniformity and reducing subjectivity in assessment. • These innovative tools can help teachers to easily identify who all need additional supervised training and who can be trusted to perform procedures independently. This process can be quantitatively monitored and certified by a unit of professional activity called EPA (Entrustable Professional Activity). 11 , 21 When a trainee can be trusted to perform a professional activity at the expected level of independence, safely and effectively, with professional attitudes in a holistic clinical context, the EPA is deemed to be completed. The ESC curriculum uses the EPAs for assessing a trainee repetitively during their training period. We in India, can also think of using simulation or mannequin-based models for assessment of procedural-based competencies during the course of training or at the time of exit examinations. • Adaptive learning : Medical schools in the American Medical Association''s Accelerating Change in Medical Education initiative are in the process of developing adaptive learning curricula similar to the New England Journal of Medicine Knowledge + program with an emphasis upon active rather than passive reading . 22 , 23 Computational algorithms are used in such teaching models, that continuously adapt questions and outcomes to identify knowledge gaps. • While digital learning through E-learning platforms like Medscape, UpToDate, and AI-powered learning tools is being increasingly used, departments should also think of having guest Lectures (physical or virtual) by experts from premier institutes, including superannuated or retired senior faculty. They bring decades of clinical experience and academic wisdom apart from mentorship, career advice, and research direction. • What can NMC and NBE do in this context: o Consider signing MOUs (Memorandums of Understanding) with international organizations like ESC (European Society of Cardiology), ACC-SAP (American College of Cardiology Self-Assessment Program) and European Accreditation Councils for structured learning programs that offer updated, evidence-based content, essential for modern cardiology practice. o Think of integrating internationally accredited CMEs into DM Cardiology training so that attending online CMEs/Webinars with these organization carry credit hours , that not only ensure ongoing professional development but also offer the trainee an opportunity to score extra marks in the final assessment. • In future, personalized learning plans using AI-based assessment tools, simulation training, Video-based coaching and Personalized educational portfolios can help tailor education to each student''s strengths and weaknesses can be conceptualized (3) Departmental protocols and resident orientations • Defining departmental protocols and organizing resident orientations is another crucial area that is often neglected. Trainees in our country come from diverse backgrounds and may often not exactly know what is expected out of them in a new environment. • Familiarizing them with specific procedural and patient-centric protocols and carrying out orientation workshops can ensure standardization and uniformity of patient care, minimize medical errors, assign clear roles and responsibilities and help them gain clarity about the academic expectations of the department. • The trainees should be well aware that their presentations of journal clubs, seminars and case discussions shall be assessed and marked by the faculty members. Quarterly or six-monthly mock examinations should be held to prepare the trainees for the final exit examinations and appropriate weightage should be given to these assessments which should count towards the final internal assessment. These methodologies help the trainees to remain informed of their performances on a regular basis and any lacunae, if noted, can be corrected proactively, prior to the final examination. (4) Research: The NMC regulations mention that a student of a degree course in broad specialties/super specialties would be required to present one poster presentation ( or) to read one paper at a national/Zonal/state conference of the respective specialty ( or) to have one research paper published/accepted for publication as the first author in the journal of the respective specialty to make him eligible to appear in the post-graduate degree examination. Although stipulated as mandatory, the quality of research is at best modest and rarely original. To improve the quality of research undertaken by DM trainees in India, structured reforms in curriculum, mentorship, funding, and evaluation are needed : • Include research methodology and biostatistics training in the DM curriculum and have regular workshops on scientific writing, research study designs, data analysis, manuscript writing, and ethical research practices. • Dedicated Mentorship : Experienced faculty members should guide research projects with maximum interdisciplinary collaboration especially with basic sciences, AI/ML experts, and global research networks. Departments should utilize the skills of retired and superannuated faculty to mentor trainees in clinical trials, research papers and grant writing. • Actively seek funding support: Collaborate up with ICMR, DST, DBT, and international funding agencies to provide dedicated grants for DM trainees. • Protected Research Time and regular research audits – Although allocation of dedicated research hours per week without clinical interruptions may be difficult to achieve in a tightly packed posting schedule, a viable alternative is to have 3 monthly research project presentation . Such regular research audit ensures timely compliance prior to final submission of manuscripts. Faculty mentors should make all efforts to have the trainees'' dissertation/thesis published in an indexed international or national journal before their final exams. This will motivate some of the students to join academia after their training. • Create a National Research Repository – akin to a central database for DM research projects, ensuring continuity and knowledge-sharing. The "SCAI-AV Gandhi Excellence in Cardiology" awards program is an initiative by the Society for Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions (SCAI) that recognizes outstanding doctoral dissertations in the field of cardiovascular sciences, aiming to motivate and encourage young cardiology professionals in India to conduct high-quality research. Every year the top 50 abstracts selected from all over India are published in an abstract year-book that can be used as a ready reference by Cardiology trainees to gain insights into the current research milieu in the country. (5) Exit exam pattern : The contemporary DM/DNB Cardiology examination consists of: (a) Theory and (b) Clinical/Practical and Oral parts. The four theory papers include Basic Medical Sciences (Paper 1), Clinical (Paper 2 and 3) and Recent Advances (Paper 4). The practical and oral examination that follows the theory papers, has for years consisted of: • 1 long case (which is always Rheumatic valvular heart disease) and lasts 45 min- 1 h. • 2 short cases (usually congenital or rheumatic; occasionally aortoarteritis, CP, HCM, CAD), that last about 20–30 min each. • Although an important part of the candidates'' assessment, very little time (typically less than 15–20 min pre candidate) is actually devoted for clinical spots or vignettes that are discussed in the viva-voce part (ECG, X-ray, Hemodynamics, Echo, angiography clips, IVUS/OCT/FFR, CT/MRI, nuclear scans). • Inexplicably, the dissertation/research project of the candidate is rarely ever discussed in some detail. This existing exam pattern needs a radical re-look and the emphasis of the practical examination should shift more towards management and practical clinical decision making . In this context, one should briefly examine the European and American assessment patterns . • The European examination in core cardiology (EEEC) is a joint venture of the Union of European Medical Specialists-Cardiology Section, the European Society of Cardiology (ESC), and the participating National and Affiliated Cardiac Societies. 24 It usually consists of 3 h long, 120 single-best-answer multiple-choice questions covering a spectrum of topics including (i) imaging and valvular heart disease; (ii) rhythm disorders; (iii) coronary artery disease, acute cardiovascular care, prevention, rehabilitation, and sports; and (iv) heart failure and cardiac patients in other settings. The questions describe a short clinical scenario which may contain investigation results and the questions asks about the best interpretation or course of action, followed by five possible answers. Approximately 30 % of questions include a clinical vignette section (a static image or a short video) • The examination conducted by the American Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM) is nearly similarly designed to evaluate the knowledge, reasoning and clinical judgment that are expected of a certified cardiovascular disease specialist the broad domain of the discipline. 25 Just like the European exam, this too is composed of up to 240 single-best-answer multiple-choice questions , of which approximately 40 are new questions that do not count in the examinee''s score. Most questions describe patient scenarios and the ability to make appropriate diagnostic and management decisions are assessed. Some questions require interpretation of ECG''s, intracardiac electrograms, hemodynamic recordings, chest radiographs and imaging studies. The major content categories and the approximate percentage assigned to each part for a usual exam are shown in Table 1 . • Are these examination models replicable : The Asian Pacific Society of Cardiology (APSC) approached the ESC in 2020 to pilot the EECC pattern among the 22 cardiac societies that are members of the APSC and labelled this as the APSC exit examinations. The APSC advises its examinees to take this examination and mentions that since the EECC examination is conducted with international standards and established methodologies, the pattern can be used as reference by cardiology trainers in the Asia–Pacific region. 26 Table 1. Course content categories of American board of internal medicine (ABIM). Medical Content Category % of Exam Arrhythmias 15 % Coronary Artery Disease 21.5 % Heart Failure and Cardiomyopathy 17 % Valvular Disease 15 % Pericardial Disease 4 % Congenital Heart Disease 5 % Vascular Diseases 6 % Systemic Hypertension and Hypotension 7.5 % Pulmonary Circulation Disorders 3 % Systemic Disorders Affecting the Circulatory System 4 % Normal Cardiovascular Anatomy and Physiology 2 % TOTAL 100 Open in a new tab What is the way forward for DM exit exam patterns our country : It is high time we make a conscious effort to shift from a predominantly rheumatic and valvular heart disease based practical examination to one that pays at least equal attention to other diseases like coronary artery disease, heart failure, cardiomyopathy, hypertension and arrhythmias (a spectrum that shall be more likely encountered by the trainee when in clinical practice). A suggested pattern of case content and the approximate leverage for each, is outlined in Table 2 . • Assessments should be based on both clinical skills and management approach and the future norm should be Case-based discussions (CbD), Mini-clinical evaluation exercises (mini-CEX) and Objective Structured Clinical Examination (OSCE). The OSCE especially can be patterned in the form of a circuit of short stations, each lasting 10–15 min, wherein the candidate is assessed on clinical skills, ability to formulate a diagnosis and management plan, interpreting data and communication. • Evaluation of the candidate''s procedural skills should also be included as part of the final examination. Since it would be unethical to use real patients, we should actively think about using simulation or mannequin -based models for workplace-based assessment (WBA) and Directly Observed Procedural Skills (DOPS). These methodologies represent a standardized and objective way to evaluate a trainee''s competency before he or she can be entrusted to perform procedures on real-world patients. • Although simulation - and mannequin-based WBA & DOPS can be integrated into DM/DNB Cardiology training in India, such tools require investment, training of the assessor themselves and should always complement, not replace , real patient-based DOPS and workplace assessments (6) Need for sub-specialty training : With a rapidly evolving spectrum of cardiology and a global trend towards super-specialization, general cardiology training may no longer be enough for optimal patient care, especially for patients with multiple co-morbidities and increasing complexity of care. Currently, DM Cardiology in India covers broad cardiology training , but exposure to super-specialized areas is limited. Only a handful of centres in our country offer structured fellowships in EP, HF, structural heart disease or Cardio-Oncology and more programs are needed to meet demand. Countries like the US, UK, and Europe already have structured super-specialty fellowships in these fields and as India progresses towards highly specialized cardiac care , post-DM Cardiology fellowships are not just desirable but essential. It shall ensure that our specialists are on par with global standards and would also boost research and innovation in these growing fields. Table 2. Suggested Course content categories and approximate leverage for each in the Indian context. Medical Content Category % of Exam Coronary Artery Disease 25 % Heart Failure and Cardiomyopathy 25 % Valvular Disease 25 % Congenital Heart Disease 10 % Pericardial Disease 15 % Hypertension Pulmonary Circulation disorders Arrhythmias Others TOTAL 100 Open in a new tab The existent unmet need for super-specialty training in India can be met with having cardiology sub-specialty fellowships for (i) interventional cardiology, (ii) cardiac electrophysiology, (iii) AHFTC, (iv) preventive cardiology, (v) advanced cardiac imaging (vi) structural heart disease and (vii) cardio-oncology . ( Fig. 1 ) Fig. 1. Open in a new tab 1. Conclusion Cardiology training in India needs a fundamental revamp to align with evolving medical advancements and patient needs. The current curriculum remains heavily focused on theoretical knowledge, necessitating a shift towards competency-based learning that integrates clinical decision-making, innovation, and real-world challenges . Modern teaching methodologies , such as participatory, personalized, and adaptive learning , should replace passive instruction, fostering deeper engagement and critical thinking. Simulation-based tools can enhance procedural skills, allowing trainees to gain hands-on expertise in a risk-free environment. Additionally, a robust research ecosystem is crucial to cultivate scientific inquiry and evidence-based practice. Training must also emphasize affective skills , including communication, teamwork, and ethical decision-making, which are essential for holistic patient care. Lastly, exit examinations should move beyond rote memorization, focusing on clinical acumen, problem-solving, and multidisciplinary approaches. A structured, forward-thinking reform in cardiology training is essential to produce well-rounded specialists capable of meeting India''s growing cardiovascular healthcare demands. Declaration of competing interest This is to declare that all of us are authors of the following manuscript titled “India Cardiology Training Curriculum- A Critical Reappraisal: Is it the Time to Rethink?” 1 All of us the authors have read the manuscript and take responsibility for its contents. 2 We have specifically looked at the order of authors and the name of the corresponding author and agree for the same. 3 We have taken special care towards the language and grammar of the article and certify it is correct to the best of our knowledge. 4 The manuscript is not under consideration with any other journal in part or as a whole. 5 We have no conflict of interest/we have the following conflict of interest. 6 We agree to transfer the copyright to Indian Heart Journal in case the manuscript is accepted for publication. References 1. Padmavati S. In: Textbook of Cardiology (Clinical & Historical Perspective) Chopra H.K., Nanda N.C., editors. 2013. Development of cardiology in India: are we on the right track. 3e4 9 of IHJ. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Das M.K., Kumar S., Deb P.K., Mishra S. History of cardiology in India. Indian Heart J. 2015;67:163–169. doi: 10.1016/j.ihj.2015.04.004. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. https://accr.n