with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: a meta-analysis of the EMPEROR-Reduced and DAPA-HF trials. Lancet. 2020 doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(20)31824-9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Nidorf SM, Eikelboom JW, Budgeon CA, Thompson PL. Low-dose colchicine for secondary prevention of cardiovascular disease. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2013;61(4):404–410. doi: 10.1016/j.jacc.2012.10.027. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Tardif JC, Kouz S, Waters DD, Bertrand OF, Diaz R, Maggioni AP, Pinto FJ, Ibrahim R, Gamra H, Kiwan GS, et al. Efficacy and safety of low-dose colchicine after myocardial infarction. N Engl J Med. 2019;381(26):2497–2505. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa1912388. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Haldar S, Khan HB, Boyalla V, Kralj-Hans I, Jones S, Lord J, Onyimadu O, Satishkumar A, Bahrami T, De Souza A, et al. Catheter ablation vs. thoracoscopic surgical ablation in long-standing persistent atrial fibrillation: CASA-AF randomized controlled trial. Eur Heart J. 2020 doi: 10.1093/eurheartj/ehaa658. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Lesyuk W, Kriza C, Kolominsky-Rabas P. Cost-of-illness studies in heart failure: a systematic review 2004–2016. BMC Cardiovasc Disord. 2018;18(1):74. doi: 10.1186/s12872-018-0815-3. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement Not applicable.',2,'2025-10-03 07:56:14','2025-10-03 07:56:14');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(132,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8014316','Negative impact of coronavirus on interventional cardiology fellows'' training: Let''s limit collateral damage of the pandemic.','Tarantini G, Nai Fovino L.','2021','reorganize nonemergent deferring collapsing endless preprocedural minimalist endures restructure preprocedural postprocedural tarantini nai fovino spaccarotella basso ehaa409 nazif vahl toselli mattesini khokhar pcronline whats pcronline tarantini nai fovino scotti impatto interventistica strutturale tarantini masiero nai fovino padua e009279 circinterventions','Key Points Coronavirus disease pandemic has caused a dramatic reduction of elective and urgent interventional cardiology procedures, with a negative impact on training of interventional cardiology fellows. This study showed that 95% of interventional cardiology fellows during the first wave of the COVID‐19 pandemic believe that their training would be moderately or severely impacted by the health crisis. Interventional cardiology programs need to reorganize in order to ensure safe pathways for both urgent and elective cases, primarily for patients'' care but also to grant an adequate caseload for trainees. With over 88 million cases and 2 million deaths as of January 10, 2021, the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID‐19) is having a catastrophic impact on most healthcare systems worldwide. This unprecedented pandemic has also caused a dramatic reduction in nonemergent clinical and surgical activities, including interventional cardiology procedures. In fact, with the overarching goals to preserve intensive care unit beds, anesthesiologists, ventilators and personal protective equipment, and to avoid unnecessary patient exposure to nosocomial infectious risk, many hospitals have been deferring nonurgent operations, including those in subjects with stable coronary artery disease and structural heart disease. Moreover, during the first wave of the COVID‐19 pandemic, a striking reduction (−48%) in hospital admissions for acute coronary syndromes has been observed, both for ST‐elevation (−26%) and non‐ST‐elevation (−65%) myocardial infarction. 1 Changes in ST‐elevation myocardial infarction protocols with a more frequent use of thrombolysis have also been reported, particularly in non‐Western countries. For these reasons, procedure volumes of most catheterization laboratories around the world significantly decreased, thereby reducing the caseload of interventional cardiology fellows. In this issue, Gupta et al 2 report the results of a survey performed at 14 interventional cardiology training hospitals in the New York metropolitan area, which has long been the epicenter of the COVID‐19 pandemic in North America. Not surprisingly, all programs reported a significant decrease in their catheterization laboratory procedural volumes, with one out of five program directors expecting their fellows to perform <250 percutaneous coronary interventions during their year of training. These results were caused not only by the reduction in interventional procedures, but also by the reallocation of cardiology fellows to COVID‐19 units to help preventing many hospitals from collapsing under the endless tide of patients admitted with respiratory distress. The findings of this survey are similar to another European experience 3 reporting a drop in catheterization laboratory activities over 50% in two thirds of centers, with half of interventional cardiology fellows revealing a reduction of the involvement in the procedures by the tutor in order to become more time‐efficient and to spare personal protective equipment in times of resources constraints. Interestingly, both program directors and interventional cardiology trainees of advanced second year structural heart intervention fellowship were less concerned by the impact of COVID‐19 pandemic on the ability to achieve adequate technical skills. These results are quite surprising, considering European data showing a significant drop (79% reduction) in structural heart interventions volume during the first wave of the pandemic, which was particularly pronounced for percutaneous mitral valve repair, left atrial appendage occlusion and patent foramen ovale closure (over 90% reduction) as compared to transcatheter aortic valve replacement (TAVR) (69% reduction). 4 Advanced interventional cardiology fellows might be more confident because they have already completed a 12 month of interventional training, but also considering that a significant proportion of structural heart intervention skills lay in preprocedural imaging and heart‐team discussion (less impacted by the pandemic), and technical procedural aspects might be perceived as secondary. Moreover, while other structural heart interventions are more likely to be postponed (as they require intubation and transesophageal echocardiography, which pose higher risk of COVID‐19 infection for the structural heart disease team and utilize constrained resources), the pandemic (together with the extension of TAVR indications to low‐risk patients) might have the paradoxical effect of increasing the number of TAVR, which is less resource‐consuming as compared to surgical aortic valve replacement. In fact, the majority of TAVR procedures can be safely performed with a minimalist percutaneous approach under conscious sedation, frequently without need for intensive care unit recovery and with next day hospital discharge. The pandemic led training programs to evolve by including a more extensive use of telemedicine, web‐conferences and simulation workshops, but none of these can substitute real‐life clinical practice in the catheterization laboratory. As the health crisis endures, it is our duty to find ways to ensure adequate patient care but also to reduce the collateral damage of the pandemic in terms of negative impact on education of future interventional cardiologists generations. To this regard, training hospitals need to restructure their practice. Implementation of formal COVID‐19 protocols with extensive use of swabs and serological testing before admission are needed to minimize the risk of in‐hospital outbreaks while ensuring appropriate and timely treatment of patients. 5 Structural heart intervention programs will need to be reorganized to minimize hospital stay (optimizing preprocedural, periprocedural, and postprocedural care), preferably without intensive care unit admission. Less‐invasive imaging tools such as intracardiac echocardiography or micro‐transesophageal echocardiography might be used in order to reduce the need for anesthesiologists'' involvement thus minimizing infrastructure requirements. As the authors pointed out in their article, despite the negative impact on procedural volumes, this unprecedented crisis provided fellows the chance to discover invaluable skills to become better doctors, such as cooperation, adaptation and, most importantly, empathy. “A good physician treats the disease, the great physician treats the patient who has the disease”—Dr. William Osler. CONFLICT OF INTEREST The authors declare no conflict of interest. Tarantini G, Nai Fovino L. Negative impact of coronavirus on interventional cardiology fellows'' training: Let''s limit collateral damage of the pandemic. Catheter Cardiovasc Interv. 2021;97:206–207. 10.1002/ccd.29479 Expert Article Analysis for: Impact of the COVID‐19 pandemic on interventional cardiology fellowship training in the New York metropolitan area: A perspective from the United States epicenter REFERENCES 1. de Rosa S, Spaccarotella C, Basso C, et al. Reduction of hospitalizations for myocardial infarction in Italy in the COVID‐19 era. Eur Heart J. 2020;41(22):2083‐2088. 10.1093/eurheartj/ehaa409. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Gupta T, Nazif TM, Vahl TP, et al. Impact of the COVID‐19 pandemic on interventional cardiology fellowship training in the New York metropolitan area: a perspective from the United States epicenter. Catheter Cardiovasc Interv. 2021;97:201‐205. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Toselli M, Mattesini A, Khokhar A, et al. The impact of COVID‐19 pandemic on interventional cardiologists in training. A Prospective Online European Survey. https://www.pcronline.com/News/Whats-new-on-PCRonline/2020/impact-COVID-19-pandemic-interventional-cardiologists-training . Accessed January 10, 2021. 4. Tarantini G, Nai Fovino L, Scotti A, et al. Impatto della pandemia da COVID‐19 sulla cardiologia interventistica strutturale in Italia [impact of COVID‐19 pandemic on structural heart interventions in Italy]. G Ital Cardiol (Rome). 2020;21(11):45‐47. 10.1714/3487.34672. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Tarantini G, Masiero G, Nai Fovino L. Impact of a 10 rules protocol on COVID‐19 hospital‐related transmission: insights from Padua university hospital, Italy. Circ Cardiovasc Interv. 2020;13(5):e009279. 10.1161/circinterventions.120.009279. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',536,'2025-10-03 07:56:26','2025-10-03 07:56:26');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(133,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11935658','What Makes an Effective Chief of Pediatric Cardiology: Insights From Chiefs of North American Pediatric Programs.','Kim ME, Franklin W, Dubin AM, Kim JJ, Sanatani S, Kumar RK, Srivastava S, Kimball TR, Lewin M, Rossano J, McMahon CJ.','2024','resounding nonstandard acronyms silo mentalities espoused chairing nebulous crumlin subdivisions visionary visionary negotiator servant trenches revolve embodying folks constructively servant patience humble servant compass stipulations handing archetype servant facile adept visionary swayed lonely instilling hone jah3 e036908 asnes carell bartz berul breinholt sheila earing chicagoland fricker gaies glatz lars grosse wortmann jacqueline kreutzer mahle larry markham shelley penny puchalski ralphe romp schowengerdt ssm glennon harinder rainbow sethuraman swaminathan towbin bonheur jefferies referees ahajournals lederer tauben tauben walpole hazzard nolke penny s00246 caddell mccrossan sands kutty latson danford imager interventionalist midttun decentralised s1744133106003045 rossano penny ppedcard sendzikaite jegatheeswaran madjalany hjortdal redington asoodar tretter s1047951122003316 duignan heuristics s00246 duignan keeffe s00246 meadows hazime fore e2335529 yedidia capers berlacher e018893 ezeoke ogueri s00246 usman siddiqi ayub acob figueredo eshtehardi jaimes dorbala gillam e001967 openhrt balasubramanian pasquali cousino lowery ansong bauser heaton choueiter e032837 dunne zuhlke penny jacadv jah3 e036908','Abstract Background Although the position of a pediatric cardiology chief is often seen as the apex of one''s academic career, its roles, responsibilities, and qualifications are not well defined in the literature. This study was done to gain further insight of the role and attributes of a pediatric cardiology chief by surveying those who are currently serving the position across North American centers. Methods and Results This was a mixed‐methods study with a survey given to North American pediatric cardiology chiefs at programs with fellowship training programs. The survey was a semistructured questionnaire that was independently reviewed by 3 cardiologists. Smaller private practice groups and nonacademic programs were excluded. Survey inventory included items rated on a 5‐point Likert scale, open‐ended prompts, and targeted questions. A total of 40 of 65 (62%) pediatric cardiology chiefs responded to the survey. Respondents identified key chief attributes included communication skills, honesty/transparency, and conflict‐resolution skills. Likert scale data demonstrated participants were satisfied with their position, although many reported growing concerns of increased demands from administration, faculty, and pressures of program performance in the current era. There is also a noted paucity of diversity among those serving in leadership positions within pediatric cardiology, which was acknowledged by survey respondents. Conclusions We gathered information directly from current North American pediatric cardiology chiefs examining the current era of its role. There are resounding themes on the emphasis for communication, honesty, conflict resolution, and mentorship. Future studies should examine faculty perceptions and a global perspective of the role. Keywords: chief, collaboration, congenital heart disease, leadership, pediatric cardiology program Subject Categories: Health Services, Ethics and Policy, Pediatrics Nonstandard Abbreviations and Acronyms DEI diversity, equity, and inclusion Clinical Perspective. What Is New? Appointment as a department chief is one of the highest achievements in pediatric cardiology; little clarity is published in terms of the role''s responsibilities and qualifications for those best fit to lead, and there is also ambiguity in understanding what attributes are most important in determining a successful chief. We surveyed current pediatric cardiology chiefs of North American programs to better understand these areas. What Are the Clinical Implications? Future studies and insights should examine opinions from faculty members, chiefs from other countries, and other subspecialty division leads to determine the generalizability of these data. The appointment as chief of pediatric cardiology represents the pinnacle in the career of most pediatric cardiologists. This is not dissimilar from other medical subspecialties. 1 , 2 , 3 However, little is documented in the literature about what the role entails, which attributes are required, and who is qualified to lead. Chiefs set the tone within departments, create the culture, and have critical roles in mentoring and supporting faculty and trainees, all while providing oversight and excellence in the provision of clinical care, research, and teaching. 4 Over time, pediatric cardiology departments have grown significantly with evolving priorities and goals focusing on clinical practice expansion, revenue targets, achieving excellence in patient care delivery and outcomes, and managing the demands with the corporate suite on optimizing resource use and expense reduction. 5 , 6 The growth in pediatric cardiology centers has resulted in greater development of subspeciality groups. This type of expansion is complicated by the inevitable development of silo mentalities within those same groups. 4 The chief role must facilitate these relationships and build an effective team between all the stakeholders, including cardiology, cardiac surgery, anaesthesiology, intensive care, nursing, and allied health care. The health care model in the United States is unique as a private health care model, which contrasts with many public or mixed care models espoused in Canada, Europe, and beyond. 7 , 8 Given the multiple tasks demanded of the chief (conflict resolution, managing uncertainty, and chairing multidisciplinary meetings) 9 , 10 , 11 , 12 , 13 , 14 and the knowledge gap on this topic in the literature, this study sought to explore the demands of the job, which personal characteristics and attributes are vital to succeed, lessons learned during the chiefs'' tenure, changes in the job over the years, increasing challenges in the job, and, most importantly, the legacy that chiefs wanted to leave behind after their time in charge. To gain greater insight into these somewhat nebulous areas, we went directly to the source: the voices of the chiefs themselves. We specifically examined these research questions in the North American setting, where the pediatric cardiology chief role is well established, formally recognized, and an integral part of the performance of the cardiology team within the heart center. METHODS Ethical approval was waived by the Ethics Department at Children''s Health Ireland, Crumlin, Dublin, Ireland, as this was a survey‐based study. This was a mixed‐methods study with grounded theory using both quantitative and qualitative assessment using a semistructured questionnaire, which was approved by 3 independent pediatric cardiologists. The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. The survey was created in SurveyMonkey to aid ease of completion by the respondents. Invitations to participate in the survey were distributed by email to all pediatric cardiology chiefs at training centers identified in the Electronic Residency Application Service online system (Data S1 ). We excluded small private practice groups, which may not have the traditional chief structure, and we wanted to sample centers that participate in training fellows and early career faculty. Need for consent to perform this survey‐based study was waived by the Ethics Department at Children''s Health Ireland. Consent was requested from all respondents to complete the survey and to facilitate the results of the study being disseminated in a peer‐reviewed pediatric cardiology journal. On initial survey data review with the author group, a supplemental survey was designed and approved, which examined further questions on diversity, equity, and inclusion (DEI) within the departments and obtaining additional demographic information. We prioritized including items on inclusion of gender reporting both from participants as well as reporting the gender composition of current faculty and subdivision leaders. There were also additional items querying the current DEI efforts from participants. Participant gender identification was self‐reported as a free‐text option within the survey. Statistical Analysis Demographics and descriptive statistics, including Likert scale responses from the survey, were summarized by medians with interquartile ranges and frequencies with percentages, as appropriate. Within the survey, participants ranked items that were then calculated by means with SDs. The open‐ended prompts were manually coded by 2 independent reviewers, which then were compiled into themes that emerged from the data. RESULTS Demographics The survey was distributed to chiefs in 65 pediatric cardiology centers across North America. Sixty centers were in the United States and 5 were in Canada. The demographics of the chiefs included number of years graduated as a pediatric cardiologist, number of years serving as pediatric cardiology chief, and whether this was their first chief position (Table 1 ). In total, 40 chiefs (62%) responded. Table 1. Participant Demographics Item Participants (n=40) Program location, % (n) United States 98 (39) Canada 2 (1) Time in practice, median (IQR), y 21 (17.5–25.5) Time served as chief, median (IQR), y 5 (2–10) First time position, % (n) 87.5 (35) Open in a new tab IQR indicates interquartile range. A supplemental survey was conducted, of which 28 chiefs (70%) responded from the initial response group (Table 2 ). There is a high predominance of male pediatric cardiology chiefs, and based on responses, ≈30% of the department faculty are female. In terms of subdivision chiefs (12 in total) on average 25% of the subdivision leads are also female. Center surgical volume from respondents demonstrated a wide range, and overall all subdivisions were represented across all programs. Most programs are classified as an academic division under the Department of Pediatrics (53%) versus those that are of an independent service line altogether (29%) or another model not listed (18%). Similarly, most respondents also stated the reporting structure in their department is directly to the Department Chair of Pediatrics (71%). Table 2. Supplemental Survey Items Item Participants (n=28) Academic rank, % (n) Professor 93 (26) Associate professor 7 (2) Gender, male, % (n) 93 (26) Cardiopulmonary bypass cases a year, % (n) 101–250 29 (8) 251–400 39 (11) 401–600 11 (3) 601–800 18 (5) 801–1000 3 (1) Department faculty size, median (IQR) 26 (26–40) Number of female faculty, median (IQR) 10 (10–21) Number of female subdivision chiefs, median (IQR) 3 (3–5) Subspecialties under pediatric cardiology, % (n) Adult congenital cardiology 86 (24) Advanced imaging (CT, MRI, 3D echo) 96 (27) Cardiac anesthesia 64 (18) Cardiac catheterization 100 (28) Cardiac intensive care 79 (22) Cardiovascular surgery 75 (21) Echocardiography 100 (28) Electrophysiology 100 (28) Fetal cardiology 100 (28) Heart failure/transplant 93 (26) Pulmonary hypertension 93 (26) Preventative cardiology 64 (18) Open in a new tab CT indicates computed tomography; 3D echo, 3‐dimensional echocardiography; IQR, interquartile range; and MRI, magnetic resonance imaging. Key Attributes of Effective Chiefs One portion of the survey tasked respondents to rank a list of traits in order of importance (Table 3 ). In summary, there were clear categories of traits established on the basis of the frequency of responses: Category 1 can be summarized as “fundamental attributes of an effective chief,” including excellent communication skills, conflict‐resolution skills, honesty/transparency, and focus on faculty development and program building. The next level includes attributes that delve deeper into humility, equitable treatment of staff, management skills, self‐awareness, and changing program culture. Following that level included being a visionary, promoting psychological safety, and academic excellence. At the bottom of the ranking includes teaching ability, negotiation skills, and professional reputation (Figure 1 ). Table 3. Average Ranking of Traits Important for Pediatric Cardiology Chief Role Item Average rank (SD) Communication skills 2.8 (1.9) Honesty 3.4 (2.8) Hard working 6.3 (3.4) Equitable treatment 6.5 (2.9) Management skills 6.7 (3.7) Humility 7.6 (4.1) Self‐awareness 7.8 (3.4) Conflict resolution 7.9 (3.6) Visionary 8.1 (4.6) Promoting psychological safety 8.9 (4.0) Effective negotiator 9.4 (3.8) Decisive 9.7 (3.7) Academic excellence 10.0 (3.3) Teacher 12.2 (2.9) Reputation 12.9 (3.0) Open in a new tab 1=Most important, and 14=least Important. Figure 1. Schema of important attributes and duties of a pediatric cardiology chief by frequency. Open in a new tab Likert Scale Responses The survey items rated on a 5‐point Likert scale can be summarized in Figures 2A and 2B . Respondents overall reported satisfaction of their role as cardiology chief, and only a minority reported the job becoming more difficult as time progresses forward. Several items tasked respondents with evaluating the current landscape of pediatric cardiology practice relative to the start of their tenure, of which a majority expressed that expectations have increased across the board from patients, faculty, and hospital systems. There is also commentary on the change of work ethic around the faculty and trainees that were observed; when prompted for clarification, several responses indicated a newer emphasis on “work‐life balance” and “wellness” for faculty and that duty hour changes and program expectations changed more than the actual quality of effort from trainees. Finally, there is a strong emphasis from respondents that felt programs focused too much on quantifying center performance and center rankings (ie, U.S. News and World Report ). Figure 2. The 5‐point Likert scale survey responses for the chief position and perceptions of the role. Open in a new tab A , Statements regarding role satisfaction and job expectations. B , Statements regarding pressures on program performance and generational changes. The supplemental survey items were also rated on a 5‐point Likert scale, which is summarized in Figure 3 . There was overwhelming agreement that expectations from the pediatric cardiology chief included efforts focused on DEI, research, and community support. Figure 3. The 5‐point Likert scale supplemental survey responses. Open in a new tab DEI indicates diversity, equity, and inclusion. Qualitative Prompts and Themes We asked a series of open‐ended questions to ascertain what traits are most important in becoming a successful pediatric cardiology chief, what did they learn, what did they regret, and what is needed for the role itself. Three main themes emerged from the identified coded data, which include the following: (1) The chief role is highly complex, requiring a diverse set of skills and traits. (2) Essential attributes for a chief include honesty, communication, and a strong work ethic. (3) Leading as a “master servant” is crucial to the success of the patients and faculty. The first theme illustrated that the chief position is multifaceted and requires adaptability and resilience to navigate multiple disciplines (ie, cardiovascular surgery, anesthesiology, cardiology, and critical care medicine), to direct financial and administrative decisions, and to develop faculty and staff within their own division. Several responses highlighted the learning curve of business and administrative skills; some suggested that formal training in these areas would also be of benefit. Faculty mentorship and development was frequently mentioned as another area that would benefit from formal training. …particularly leading by example. I think the best chiefs demonstrate what is desired in terms of work ethic, engagement with clinical activities, and overall attitude. …the chief needs to be an example of the work ethic they expect. They need to behave professionally, work in the trenches to demonstrate solidarity with faculty, and promote high ethical standards. The second theme entailed that the chief must have several intrinsic traits to be successful. Several of these characteristics revolve around communication skills, including the ability to manage conflict resolution, motivation, negotiation, and listening. Other notable areas include resiliency, adaptability, confidence, and transparency. There is also some mention (but to a much lesser degree) of emphasis on clinical skills, academic productivity, and reputation among peers. Communication skills, treating everyone else (no matter level/rank) with dignity and respect, embodying the desired culture, inspiring others to follow along. …ability to be a player‐coach…folks realise you are not asking them to do something you wouldn''t do. Effective delegation of responsibilities in a way that enables others to assume leadership roles…ability to not react but rather interact constructively…ability to have hard conversations in a timely fashion. Finally, the third theme highlighted the concept of servant leadership, which entails a combination of personal traits and extrinsic features. This is highlighted by the repeated emphasis on humility, honesty, patience, and empathy. Interestingly, this also manifests in the roles of the chief including aspects of mentorship, faculty recruitment/promotion, team building, and developing a well‐functioning and communicative division. Honesty, integrity, humble but confident. You must truly put the other person success and advancement before your own. Be a servant leader. Keep your moral compass pointing north. Listen. When specifically asked about methods in which to promote high quality research among faculty, the most common items include providing protected time, securing funding and resources, and development of a research infrastructure to support investigators. DISCUSSION We surveyed two‐thirds of the North American pediatric cardiology chiefs working at academic cardiac centers with fellowship training programs. To characterize the role of the chief position, respondents highlighted the complexity of the job in terms of the wide array of responsibilities and interpersonal skills required. Communication skills and integrity were most consistently reported as the most important aspects of the position both in terms of ranking traits (Table 3 ) and from qualitative prompts. It is also important to highlight that the respondents were also largely satisfied with their career choice and used the survey as a point of reflection. The survey participants illustrated the nuanced pressures of the position through the increased pressures to lead and perform. Although specific causes are not objectively outlined, the expansion of cardiac surgery programs and increased volume of patient referrals and outcomes‐based metrics are certainly plausible reasons. The Likert scale items highlighted the trends of both programs and faculty/fellow changes over time, and although there was no overwhelming agreement in facing pressures from these factors, there certainly is a significant group of respondents who felt expectations from faculty, trainees, and administrators to be higher in the current era. The pediatric cardiology chief role, as complex and intense as it continues to be, has several stipulations that are unclear when it comes to succession planning and term limits. Respondents on average have been in the position for 5 years (interquartile range, 2–10 years) and have overwhelmingly stressed the importance of term limits as well as thoughtful transitions of the role (Table 4 ). These types of ambiguities may also be obstacles in individuals who may be interested in the position given there are no clear policies or cultural practices in institutions at large for the duration and transition of the role. Although the overall satisfaction is high for respondents who are currently serving as chiefs, several commented as their position is ending that there is a sense of relief and anxiety because of the thoughts of handing off their work as well as the level of intensity and rigor that encompassed the role. Table 4. Responses Pertaining to Chief Position Longevity and Succession Item Response, % (n) Yes Depends No Should the chief position rotate every 5–10 y? 72.5 (29) 17.5 (7) 10 (4) Do you believe succession planning is important as a part of the chief role? 95 (38) … 5 (2) If you started your career over, would you serve as chief again? 85 (34) 7.5 (3) 7.5 (3) Open in a new tab Our decision to expand the initial survey yielded additional insights into DEI practices throughout the various cardiac centers. Despite most departments having at least 30% female faculty, there is a much smaller representation at the leadership level within the subdivision and cardiology chief positions. These data support other findings relating to gender and race throughout the field, with women and minorities continuing to be underrepresented in areas of leadership. 15 , 16 , 17 , 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 , 22 , 23 Although there is greater awareness of this issue and with ongoing efforts to promote underrepresented groups, more work needs to be done. This survey highlights the need for frequent review of how faculty promotion and leadership positions are allocated and should be evaluated frequently to ensure biases are being addressed. Specifically, it would be important for programs to evaluate their hiring and promotional practices to identify any bias that can be assessed by third‐party consultants and advocates for diversity, equity, and inclusion. Another important aspect should include improvements in the support for faculty mentorship and professional development to encourage and identify leadership candidates within the field. Another important factor would be the inclusion of benchmarks for DEI in ranking programs (ie, U.S. News and World Report ), which would influence intentional transformation and supporting development of an infrastructure to support the same. Finally, and perhaps the most interesting data from the survey include the intrinsic and extrinsic traits of a pediatric cardiology chief that were highlighted. From these responses, it is plausible to create an archetype of the “ideal” chief: a servant leader, empathetic, communicative, resilient, facile with interprofessional matters, adept in financial and administrative tasks, a strong mentor, and a visionary who can build a strong cardiac program (Figure 2 ). Although this list is not finite, it is sufficient to conclude that the role is a highly diverse and multifaceted position that requires an individual who not only must possess the skills but the passion to lead. Limitations There are several limitations to this survey‐based study. The questionnaire, although reviewed by 3 cardiologists, was not a validated instrument as we could not find such a validated tool for assessing chiefs of pediatric cardiology divisions. We surveyed the chiefs themselves as we believed they have a unique perspective on their own role; however, the voice of faculty may have been just as informative. Also, although we investigated potential disparities with regard to gender in leadership roles, we did not ask questions on other DEI domains, including race. We acknowledge this is important and would recommend future studies would expand on this topic. We hope to study their perceptions of the qualities required in an effective chief in the future. We focused on academic pediatric cardiology units around North America and did not survey small private practice cardiac units as we wanted to hear the experience from chiefs in centers committed to education and training of faculty and trainees. Although we attempted to contact every unit in North America, there may have been a small number we could not reach because of changes in chief or an interim chief being appointed. There is also a limitation to the generalizability of our survey data as we did not include private practice programs. In addition, a more robust future survey might include greater detail around the division chief skill set required to (1) ensure equity, diversity, and inclusion, (2) manage an efficient and effective business, (3) develop a high‐quality research program, and (4) ensure that the referring provider community is well served. Future studies can also examine the role of the pediatric cardiology chief position from the perspective of faculty and from chiefs globally. Proposals Pediatric cardiology divisions should direct their focus to the high‐frequency reported attributes, including excellent communication skills, honesty, strong work ethic, and humility. Programs should not be swayed by reputation and numbers of articles published or grants secured. These latter abilities were not mentioned as key attributes to being an effective successful chief. Individuals who become chiefs should be considered regardless of race, gender, or age. However, there is a clear preponderance in North America of White male chiefs. Further insight is needed within the selection process of program leadership to address any bias or barriers in terms of desired attributes or leadership styles to promote increased diversity in these crucial roles. Many programs are high‐quality programs with excellent care provided to patients and families. Rating agency rankings do not necessarily reflect program excellence, and most chiefs believed there is too much focus on rankings. The chief role can be extremely fulfilling and full of moments of joy; however, it can prove an isolated and lonely position. Fostering a safe space either in person or online for chiefs to communicate their challenges in a psychologically safe space would be beneficial. Identifying potential chiefs of the future and instilling key skill sets in them early in their career may potentially optimize the performance of cardiac units, thereby improving outcomes for patients and ensuring there are opportunities for succession planning. Term limits can be explored among centers to promote program diversity and opportunities for improvement. Provision of chiefs with “professional coaching” has the potential to allow early to mid career cardiology chiefs with a route to hone skills and allow for opportunities for improvement. CONCLUSIONS Pediatric cardiology chiefs have a complex and demanding role requiring multiple skill sets. Communications skills, honesty, hard work, and humility remain the cornerstone of characteristics key to being an effective chief. Reputation and academic excellence may be less important as an effective chief and should not be the primary focus of search committees. Facilitating greater communication between chiefs themselves, potentially through training days, may foster a greater collaborative environment and help mitigate stresses associated with the position. Sources of Funding None. Disclosures None. Supporting information Data S1 JAH3-13-e036908-s001.pdf (204.4KB, pdf) Acknowledgments We are grateful to Dr Jeremy Asnes, Yale School of Medicine; Dr H. Scott Baldwin, Monroe Carell Jr Children''s Hospital at Vanderbilt; Dr Peter Bartz, Medical College of Wisconsin Affiliated Hospitals; Dr Charles I. Berul, Children''s National Hospital; Dr John P. Breinholt III, Penn State Health Children''s Hospital; Dr Sheila J. Carroll, Weill Cornell Medical Center; Dr Jeffrey Delaney, Children''s Nebraska/University of Nebraska Medical Center; Dr Michael G. Earing, Advocate Children''s/Chicagoland Children''s Health Alliance; Dr F. Jay Fricker, University of Florida; Dr Michael Gaies, Cincinnati Children''s Hospital Medical Center; Dr Andrew C. Glatz, Washington University School of Medicine/St. Louis Children''s Hospital; Dr Eric M. Graham, Medical University of South Carolina; Dr Lars Grosse‐Wortmann, Oregon Health Sciences University; Dr Jonathan Johnson, Mayo Clinic; Dr Jacqueline Kreutzer, UPMC Children''s Hospital of Pittsburgh; Dr Ian H. Law, University of Iowa; Dr Yung R. Lau, Children''s of Alabama/University of Alabama at Birmingham; Dr Nicolas Madsen, Children''s Health UT Southwestern; Dr William Mahle, Children''s Healthcare of Atlanta; Dr Larry W. Markham, Riley Hospital for Children/Indiana University School of Medicine; Dr Shelley Miyamoto, Children''s Hospital of Colorado; Dr Robert Pass, Mount Sinai; Dr Daniel Penny, Texas Children''s Hospital; Dr George Porter, University of Rochester; Dr Michael Puchalski, Johns Hopkins All Children''s; Dr J. Carter Ralphe, University of Wisconsin–Madison; Dr Robert Romp, Children''s of Alabama/University of Alabama at Birmingham; Dr Kenneth O. Schowengerdt Jr, SSM Health Cardinal Glennon Children''s Hospital/Saint Louis University School of Medicine; Dr Harinder R. Singh, Rainbow Babies and Children''s Hospital; Dr Christopher S. Snyder, Virginia Commonwealth; Dr Sethuraman Swaminathan, University of Miami/Jackson Health System; Dr Jeffrey A. Towbin, Le Bonheur Children''s University of Tennessee. This manuscript was sent to John L. Jefferies, MD, MPH, Guest Editor, for review by expert referees, editorial decision, and final disposition. Supplemental Material is available at https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/suppl/10.1161/JAHA.124.036908 For Sources of Funding and Disclosures, see page 8. References 1. Lederer E. Leadership as a division chief of nephrology. Adv Chronic Kidney Dis. 2018;25:480–484. doi: 10.1053/j.ackd.2018.08.005 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Tauben D. Interview with David Tauben: University of Washington, chief of the division of pain medicine. Pain Manag